# Untitled

## Electrochemical \(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\) reduction to \(\mathbf{C}_{2+}\) products using Cu-based electrocatalysts: A review
Touqeer Ahmad\(^{1}\), Shuang Liu\(^{1}\), Muhammad Sajid\(^{1}\), Ke Li\(^{1}\), Mohsin Ali\(^{1}\), Liang Liu\(^{2}\), and Wei Chen\(^{1}\) (✉)
\(^{1}\) Department of Applied Chemistry, School of Chemistry and Materials Science, Hefei National Research Center for Physical Sciences at the Microscale, University of Science and Technology of China, Hefei 230026, China  \(^{2}\) Department of Ophthalmology, School of Medicine, Stanford University, Palo Alto, CA 94304, USA
## ABSTRACT
With the disruptive carbon cycle being blamed for global warming, the plausible electrocatalytic \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) reduction reaction \((\mathsf{C O}_{2}\mathsf{R R})\) to form valuable \(\mathsf{C}_{2+}\) hydrocarbons and feedstock is becoming a hot topic. Cu-based electrocatalysts have been proven to be excellent \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\mathsf{R R}\) alternatives for high energy value-added products in this regard. However, the selectivity of \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\mathsf{R R}\) to form \(\mathsf{C}_{2+}\) products via Cu-based catalysts suffers from a high overpotential, slow reaction kinetics, and low selectivity. This review attempts to discuss various cutting-edge strategies for understanding catalytic design such as Cu-based catalyst surface engineering, tuning Cu bandgap via alloying, nanocatalysis, and the effect of the electrolyte and pH on catalyst morphology. The most recent advances in in situ spectroscopy and computational techniques are summarized to fully comprehend reaction mechanisms, structural transformation/degradation mechanisms, and crystal facet loss with subsequent effects on catalyst activity. Furthermore, approaches for tuning Cu interactions are discussed from four key perspectives: single-atom catalysts, interfacial engineering, metal-organic frameworks, and polymer-incorporated materials, which provide new insights into the selectivity of \(\mathsf{C}_{2+}\) products. Finally, major challenges are outlined, and potential prospects for the rational design of catalysts for robust \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\mathsf{R R}\) are proposed. The integration of catalytic design with mechanistic understanding is a step forward in the promising advancement of \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\mathsf{R R}\) technology for industrial applications.
## KEYWORDS
electrocatalysis, \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) reduction, \(\mathsf{C}_{2+}\) hydrocarbon, Cu materials, nanostructures
## 1 Introduction
The industrial revolution in the nineteenth century led to the use of carbon-rich fossil fuels, resulting in the excessive release of carbon dioxide \(\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)\) into the atmosphere. The dramatic increase in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) concentrations has resulted in adverse climate changes, ocean acidification, and sea-level rise, all of which have exacerbated severe environmental issues and have posed unavoidable threats to living species [1–5]. As of March 2021, the atmospheric \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) concentration had risen to \(417.64~\mathrm{ppm}\), far exceeding the upper safety limit of \(350~\mathrm{ppm}\) [6, 7]. As a result of such a closed loop for carbon footprint, there is a possibility of shifting current energy generation away from fossil fuels and toward renewable energy sources such as solar and wind [8–10]. However, the energy fraction obtained from renewable sources is only \(30\%\), which is insufficient to completely replace fossil fuel energy production until and unless green energy is widely generated on large scales [11].
Hence capturing and conversion of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) from the source point to high energy value-added products render an alternative approach that is considered virtually more feasible [12, 13].
Electrochemical \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction reaction \((\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR})\) via renewable electricity into valuable chemicals could decrease the consumption of fossil fuels and further reduce \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) emission [14]. However, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) in its highest oxidation state \(\scriptstyle(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O})\) is highly stable and claimed \(750\,\mathrm{kJ/mol}\) of energy for its conversion [15]. \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) in aqueous electrolytes involves complex mechanisms that encompass multi-electron/proton transfer processes and several different possible reaction intermediates and products [16–18]. Until now, chemical, electrochemical, and photochemical methods have been used to investigate the conversion of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) into \(\mathrm{C}_{1}\) and \(\mathrm{C}_{2+}\) products. Such products mostly include carbon monoxide (CO) [19], methane \(\left(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\right)\) [20], methanol \(\left(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}\right)\) [21], formic acid (HCOOH) [22], alkyl carbonates [23], ethylene \(\left(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\right)\), and ethanol \(\left(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}\right)\) [24]. A general mechanism in electrocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) involves the adsorption of the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) moiety over the catalytic surface by a weak physical interaction to form \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}^{-}\), followed by a series of electron/proton transfer, resulting in the formation of various products. In addition to the variation in protonation sites, the involvement of nonelectrochemical phases initiates uncontrollable and complex reactions during the formation
of \( C_{2+} \) chemicals [24, 25]. To address this issue, efficient and robust electrocatalysts are being sought to greatly facilitate otherwise kinetically slow \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) [26].
The primary class of heterogeneous electrocatalysts tends to produce formic acid or CO via two-electron transfer [1, 14]. In general, metals with low binding affinity are prone to form a \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}^{-} \)intermediate, such as those based on Sn, Bi, and Pb, along with formic acid or formate. Conversely, Zn, Au, and Ag produce CO and tend to generate \( ^{\ast}\mathrm{COOH} \) because of their high binding affinity [27, 28]. There have been several high-quality review articles published on electrocatalytic \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) via homogeneous catalysis [29, 30], but only a few of them are devoted to heterogeneous catalysis. Prior to the development of nanotechnology and computational software, traditional metal electrocatalysts were extensively studied for \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \). Such electrocatalysts have now been replaced with a variety of novel inorganic heterogeneous Cu-based catalysts for eliciting new and robust \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \). In this regard, recent research has looked into novel pathways for controlling \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \). As a result, a timely and comprehensive review of recent advances in new heterogeneous electrocatalysts for \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) to form \( C_{2+} \) products is highly desirable.
Copper has earned the distinction of being the only catalyst capable of converting \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) into multicarbon products with high efficiency and selectivity [31, 32]. Copper and its derivatives, such as Cu alloys, Cu derived from oxides, and copper composites, have shown enormous potential for \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) into valuable chemical feedstock. Figure 1 depicts the various strategies and associated challenges for carrying out efficient \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) to yield \( C_{2+} \) products. Numerous published research articles on \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) have revealed the difficulties in fine-tuning the selectivity of \( C_{2+} \) products using Cu-based catalysis [33–35]. Furthermore, the designs of highly selective and effective Cu-based catalysts for producing high yields of multicarbon products are still being researched. In addition, the Faradaic efficiency (FE) of \( C_{2+} \) products is still far from its practical applications, requiring at least \( 70\% \) FE, whereas that of \( C_{3} \) products is limited to less than \( 10\% \) [36, 37]. Reductive coupling of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) to \( C_{2+} \) products entails heterogeneous catalysts with highly coordinated morphological and electronic properties.
During industrial applications, an efficient catalyst is typically distinguished by its long-term operation, high current density, and excellent FE [38]. There have been few studies on the structural stability of the Cu-based electrocatalyst in comparison to selectivity and performance issues during \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \). Cu catalyst operations last a few hundred hours [39], but structural degradation occurs within the first few minutes of operation [40]. Similarly, in comparison to other electrochemical reactions, the phenomena involving catalyst robustness and degradation during \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) are rarely investigated [41, 42]. As a result, there is an urgent need for a comprehensive and systematic review of Cu-based catalysts to identify factors influencing their stability and selectivity in producing \( C_{2+} \) products. There is also a need to gain fundamental understanding of electrocatalytic processes, which have been prominently featured in this study. The study presents new perspectives, challenges, and future opportunities for using Cu-based electrocatalysts to convert \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) into value-added \( C_{2+} \) chemicals.
The goal of this review is to highlight current challenges and recent advances in designing cutting-edge Cu-based catalysts for the synthesis of valuable \( C_{2+} \) hydrocarbons, as well as to gain insights into the deactivation/degradation mechanism. For
instance, in situ and operando spectroscopic and microscopic methods have been used to gain insight into the relationship among the structure, chemical state, and reactivity of size and shape-controlled ligand-free Cu nanocubes (Cu cubes) during \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \). The importance of such characterization in predicting distinct aspects such as the loss of crystal facet, morphological variation, the dominance of \( C_{2+} \) product formation over one copper facet versus others, and time-dependent consumption of catalyst performance toward the \( C_{2+} \) product has been discussed. Furthermore, the performance of Cu-derived catalysts in different electrolytes before and during the reaction, as well as the impact of their morphology on driving the \( C_{2+}/C_{1} \) ratio, has been summarized. Furthermore, the importance of such studies in gaining fundamental understanding of electrocatalytic processes has been highlighted. Finally, the study provides new perspectives on addressing challenges as well as future prospects, such as the use of single-atom catalysts (SACs), metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), Cu interface, and polymer-incorporated Cu for electrochemical \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) into value-added \( C_{2+} \) chemicals. The study emphasizes the significance of using Cu electrocatalysts to reduce greenhouse gas emissions during industrial processes (Fig. 1)
## 2 Impact of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) on the energy market
With the dramatic increase in \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) concentrations, it is critical to develop a new strategy to reduce \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) commutation in the atmosphere. Commercial carbon capture and storage technologies appear to be effective prerequisites for converting \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) molecules into high-energy valuable fuels [47]. Conversely, thermodynamic cell potential confers economic feasibility, minimum product energy requirements, and energy cost for the synthesis of specific reaction products. This notion is supported further by techno-economic analyses involving
operating costs by assuming the overpotential and FE of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) [48, 49]. Figure 2 depicts the approximate economics mapping (market price) and energetics of viable \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction products plotted against the least amount of energy required for the formation of possible products from \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\). The carbon mass is used to standardize all economic quantities. As shown by the dashed and dotted lines, the lowest possible production cost for the captured \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is \(\mathbb{S}200/\mathrm{tC}\) and electricity costs \(\mathbb{S}50/\mathrm{MWh}\) or \(\mathbb{S}20/\mathrm{MWh}\). Furthermore, the products mentioned above are \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture and electricity cost effective when compared to products below the line based on capital and other costs. Recent technological advancements have resulted in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture having an estimated cost of \(\mathbb{S}2\,000/\mathrm{tC}\) [50, 51] which exceeds the overheads of all products. Figure 2 illustrates the near-term economic viability via \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction to attain essential chemical products, such as HCOOH (0.2 MtC/yr [52]) and \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{7}\mathrm{OH}\) (0.1 MtC/yr [53]).
As a result, the search for alternative renewable energy sources to coal (4 GtC/yr [54]) and natural gas (1.4 GtC/yr [55]) continues to slow the current rate of global warming. The minimum energy requirement for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction costs, which is much higher than that of natural sources even at \(\mathbb{S}20/\mathrm{MWh}\), makes replacing fossil fuel with renewable energy economically feasible. High market prices for \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) (120 MtC/yr [56]) and \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{OH}\) (40 MtC/yr [53, 57–59]) indicate a valuable opportunity among profitable industries for significant \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) mass reduction. For instance, ethylene, a precursor material for plastics and ethylene glycol, is highly unselective, necessitating a large amount of energy over a series of repeated cycles involving steam cracking at \(750{-}950\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), followed by quenching, distillation, and recompression [60, 61]. As a result, with the least amount of energy input, such treatment produces twice as much energy as the \(\mathbb{S}50/\mathrm{MWh}\) electricity price. This, in turn, has an impact on the global market, which is 100 times the value of valuable chemicals. Similarly, another fossil fuel alternative, ethanol, has growing potential to power automobile industries, explaining its significant impact on the renewable energy market [62]. Because of its high volumetric energy density, its mass production requires little energy input \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) at \(\mathbb{S}50/\mathrm{MWh})\), resulting in much higher rates than its manufacturing price.
## 3 Copper as a unique electrocatalyst for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) to \(\mathbf{C}_{2+}\) products
The first electrocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) was reported in the 1950s [64] when Hori et al. reported hydrocarbon-based quantification of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) with \(100\%\) FE. \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) was performed on several polycrystalline metal electrodes at a constant current density of \(5\,\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) (geometric) in a \(0.5\,\mathrm{M}\,\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}\) solution. Following studies [65, 66] used similar procedures and classified the metal electrodes into four groups based on the types of reaction products. The first group includes Pb, Hg, Tl, In, Sn, Cd, and Bi catalysts that produce formate, and the second group includes Au, Ag, Zn, Pd, and Ga catalysts that produce \(\mathrm{CO}\) as a major product. The third group of metals includes Ni, Fe, Pt, and Ti, which have low \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) competency. Furthermore, these metals promote the reduction of water to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\). Cu is classified as a separate element because of its novelty in the production of multicarbon hydrocarbons, ethane, and alcohols (Fig. 3). Cu, as a nonprecious metal, can reduce \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to valuable chemicals via more than two-electron transfer mechanisms with significant FE, as shown in Table 1. It is clear that all Cu-based catalysts are capable of achieving a minimum current density of \(5\,\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}\), which other catalysts cannot achieve without a significant overpotential. Furthermore, Cu-based catalysts exhibit excellent electrocatalytic performance in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\), producing a wide range of hydrocarbons and oxygenated products at a moderate potential of \(-1.04\;\mathrm{V}\) vs. reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE). For instance, the minimal overpotential required to convert \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to a \(\mathrm{CO}\) intermediate is \(-0.6\,\mathrm{V}\) vs. RHE, whereas it is \(-1.2\;\mathrm{V}\)
for \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) to methane conversion [76, 77]. Standard reduction potential for \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{R} \) to CO and further \( C_{2+} \) hydrocarbons is given in Table 2. These results outperform selectivity phenomena in \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) over different electrodes at a single current density. It is important to note that \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) selectivity can be altered to produce different products by adjusting reaction conditions and electrode potentials. Furthermore, the selectivity phenomena of these metals have been shown to have a significant impact on \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) and hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) intermediates [78]. The exceptional role of \( \mathrm{Cu} \) in \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) in offering more than two reaction products is attributed to its negative adsorption energy for \( ^{\star}\mathrm{CO} \) as well as positive adsorption energy for \( ^{\star}\mathrm{H} \). In this regard, Hori et al. demonstrated \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) product distribution as a function of potential by performing \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) at a constant potential in a \( 0.1\,\mathrm{M} \) \( \mathrm{KHCO}_{3} \) saturated solution \( \mathrm{(pH}=6.8) \) [67]. As a result, starting from the least cathodic to the most cathodic, CO, \( \mathrm{HCOO}^{-} \), \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4} \), \( \mathrm{CH}_{4} \), CO, and \( \mathrm{HCOO}^{-} \) are produced as products with plausible potentials and maximum FE toward alkenes at a plateau of approximately \( -1.0\;\mathrm{V} \) vs. RHE. Similarly, Kuhl and colleagues [68] conducted electrolysis experiments with potentials ranging from \( -0.6 \) to \( -1.2\,\mathrm{V} \) vs. RHE. As a result, 16 products are obtained when evaluated using ultrasensitive detection techniques, and the results are in close agreement with those of Hori's work. Following the preceding studies, it is clear that the \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) conversion to valuable high-energy chemicals (e.g., alkanes, alkenes, aldehydes, and alcohols) is a hot topic, and greater control over selectivity and efficiency is possible through surface engineering and by tuning the bandgap of \( \mathrm{Cu} \) in \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) reduction.
## 3.1 The formation of the C–C bond
When \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) is performed over a \( \mathrm{Cu} \) electrode, the \( \mathrm{C}-\mathrm{C} \) bond is critical. Aside from \( \mathrm{Cu} \) catalysts, the roles of transition metals and carbon nanomaterials in \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) to produce \( C_{2+} \) compounds have also been investigated. It has been established that no catalysts other than those based on \( \mathrm{Cu} \) can effectively reduce \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) to \( C_{2+} \) products with substantial efficacy [79–82]. Several methods for improving selectivity against multiple hydrocarbons have recently been reported, including tuning the copper structure, varying the electrolyte composition, and coating an organic film over the copper surface [83, 84]. These studies demonstrate that \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) liquid state products have high energy and are inexpensive [14]. As a result, several experimental [17, 69, 85–88] and theoretical [89–93] studies have been conducted to elucidate the pathway from \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) to \( C_{2+} \). However, the subsequent transformation of the adsorbed \( \mathrm{CO} \) intermediate into various products has remained a mystery.
This study explains numerous reduction processes by referring to commonly and recently reported mechanisms, which are piqued by the diverse product spectrum after ionic liquid (IL) modification (Fig. 4) [94]. For instance, ethylene has been reported to be produced via two distinct routes. The first is the high-overpotential pathway that primarily occurs on \( \mathrm{Cu} \) (111) via sharing intermediates with the methane pathway. The second pathway, known as a low-overpotential pathway, occurs on \( \mathrm{Cu} \) (100) and is incapable of producing \( C_{1} \) products [87]. It is critical to note that the \( \mathrm{pH} \) near the electrode has a significant impact on these two pathways. The \( \mathrm{Cu} \) (111) pathway is \( \mathrm{pH} \) dependent, whereas the \( C_{2} \) pathway (during \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \)) involving \( \mathrm{Cu} \) (100) is \( \mathrm{pH} \) independent. As a result, the \( C_{2} \) pathway favors the \( \mathrm{pH} \)-dependent pathway (HER) and participates in the selective reduction of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) and \( \mathrm{CO} \) over the \( \mathrm{Cu} \) electrode.
Several experimental [17, 85–87] and theoretical [89–91] studies have identified a \( \mathrm{CO} \) dimer as a critical intermediate in the \( C_{2} \) pathway. In this regard, density functional theory (DFT) calculations in the \( \mathrm{C}-\mathrm{C} \) bond have disfavored \( \mathrm{CO} \) dimerization over more negative potentials and have suggested alternative pathways toward \( ^{\star}\mathrm{CO} \) and \( ^{\star}\mathrm{CHO} \) coupling [91–93, 95, 96]. A \( \mathrm{pH} \)-dependent decoupled proton–electron transfer has been proposed, and this dependency has been supported by theoretical and practical studies [89]. The formation of a \( \mathrm{CO} \) dimer is preferred thermodynamically, as revealed by DFT calculations, whereas theoretical studies suggest that \( \mathrm{CO} \) dimerization is structurally sensitive. To investigate molecular-level insights into the \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) mechanisms, Cai et al. [97] performed comprehensive review of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) over \( \mathrm{Cu} \) catalysts and highlighted in situ vibrational approaches including infrared, Raman, and sum frequency generation spectroscopies have been arrayed for monitoring dynamic evolution of catalyst structures, to probe reactive intermediates as well as to scrutinize the effect of local reaction environment on \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) activity. Furthermore, various reaction parameters, alkali cations, the presence of water layers, local electric field, and its formation energy have been found to favor \( \mathrm{Cu} \) (100) over \( \mathrm{Cu} \) (111) [90, 92]. A group of researchers recently investigated a hydrogenated \( \mathrm{CO} \) dimer (OCCOH) at a low potential in an \( \mathrm{LiOH} \) electrolyte using Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR), together with DFT calculations. \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) to \( C_{2+} \) species conversion has been found to occur over \( \mathrm{Cu} \) (100) via sites with square symmetry through a reductive dimerization step [86, 92, 98]. Furthermore, it has been substantiated that \( C s^{+} \) in electrolytes promotes the production of ethylene with high FE during the reduction of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) and \( \mathrm{CO} \). Conversely, the formation of a suspected intermediate in the \( \mathrm{CsOH} \) electrolyte is not observed because of its energetics. The accumulation of certain organic layers on the copper electrode results in enhanced \( C_{2+} \) product selectivity, but the mechanism of action is still ambiguous regarding local \( \mathrm{pH} \) [83, 84]. Electrolytes with low buffer strength elevate \( \mathrm{pH} \) in the electrode's vicinity, thereby improving \( C_{2+} \) product selectivity. Furthermore, during \( \mathrm{CO} \) and \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) reduction, ethylene and ethanol or acetaldehyde is formed in \( \mathrm{Cu} \) catalysis [99]. It is commonly assumed that \( C_{2+} \) species of ethanol, ethylene, and acetaldehyde share common intermediates at \( \mathrm{Cu} \) (100) until a selectivity-determining intermediate comes into play. For example, \( ^{\star}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{CHO} \) is a structure-sensitive intermediate that can be further reduced to form ethylene or ethanol [100–102].
Recent computational work has suggested that the selectivity-determining intermediates are formed earlier in the mechanism [95]. An alternative pathway for ethanol formation over a \(\mathrm{Cu-Zn}\) or \(\mathrm{Cu-Ag}\)-based electrode claims an insertion-type mechanism (CO reacting with \(\mathrm{Cu-CH}_{2}\) species). In addition, experimental studies have shown that CO dimerization can be used to explain why silver favors ethanol over ethylene formation [100]. Acetate is another \(\mathrm{C}_{2+}\) product observed during \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\), and it is assumed to form as a side product during the reaction pathway of ethylene via isomerization of a three-membered ring or coupling of different adsorbed intermediates over the catalyst surface [104, 105]. Figure 5 shows an overview of the reaction pathways toward \(\mathrm{C}_{2+}\) products, with an important caveat toward several competing pathways at high overpotentials. Cu catalysis also results in the synthesis of \(\mathrm{C}_{3+}\) hydrocarbons with FE of up to \(20\%\) [106]. Propanol and propionaldehyde are the additional \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\) hydrocarbons obtained, followed by trace amounts of hydroxyacetone, acetone, and allyl alcohol [68]. The formation of n-propanol has been confirmed via \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{C}\) coupling between CO and \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) precursors on oxide-derived Cu (OD-Cu) nanocrystals containing structure defect sites [107]. Although there has been insufficient research
on the electrocatalytic formation of \(\mathrm{C}_{3+}\) hydrocarbons via \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\), the steps of the CO insertion-type reaction appear similar in general.
## 3.2 Mechanisms of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction to \(\mathrm{C}_{3+}\) products
Electrocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) to \(\mathrm{C}_{2+}\) hydrocarbon is an intricate and multistep process. However, the exact reaction mechanism remains uncertain and is prone to alter with a plethora of reaction conditions [108]. Sculpting such heterogeneous catalytic operation, four basic steps are generally accepted [109, 110].
Step 1: The first step involves \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) chemisorption upon electrocatalyst surface, which results in partly charged \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \delta \) species via interaction with metal surface. Usually, \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) can physisorb as a linear molecule at the same time, which can then be energized to a chemisorbed form by surface defects, alkali-metal-promoted surfaces, or electrons during electrolysis.
Step 2: Later charge transfer (electron–proton coupling) facilitates \( \mathrm{C}-\mathrm{O} \) bond dissociation to form further reaction intermediates.
Step 3: Most vital steps involve formation of \( \mathrm{C}_{2+} \) species such as hydrocarbons and oxygenates through \( \mathrm{C}-\mathrm{C} \) bond formation.
Step 4: The formed products rearranged and desorbed from metal surface into the electrolytic solution.
## 3.3 Selectivity control via nanocatalysts
To date, different strategies have been adapted to manipulate the performance of \( \mathrm{Cu} \) catalysts and mainly involve nanosurface and morphological programmed engineering [44, 112–114]. These methods can reduce the overpotential toward \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) via a similar pathway and can diminish the HER kinetics [115]. Furthermore, the binding strength of the targeted intermediate is tunable using \( \mathrm{Cu} \) alloys, which significantly improves selectivity and reaction kinetics. However, more research is needed to determine the mechanism of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) over polycrystalline \( \mathrm{Cu} \) surfaces. When the \( \mathrm{Cu} \) surface is transposed to the nanoscale, the \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) reaction kinetics impeded, with a significant increase in HER kinetics [74, 116]. A reduced particle size increases the surface-to-bulk atom ratio and enriches uncoordinated sites, lowering the average coordination of surface atoms and facilitating curvature surface proliferation [117]. Furthermore, reducing the coordination number of catalytically active atoms causes an uneasy electronic structure and frequently increases reactivity. Furthermore, theoretical investigation reveals that the existence of a low coordinated atom at the catalyst surface develops a strong surface interaction with \( \mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{ad}} \) and \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) intermediates ( \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{C} \) or \( \mathrm{COOH} \)), which then triggers the Heyrovsky mechanism for \( \mathrm{H}-\mathrm{H} \) bond formation, the most favorable route toward HER. For ultrasmall particles, significant strain induced over the surface of the atom alters the d-band gap and affects the reactivity of the catalyst [118].
Tuning catalyst reactivity via material design is another method for improving or degrading reaction performance. The effect of a change in catalyst reactivity for a simple \( 2\mathrm{e}^{-} \) transfer can be evaluated by examining the optimum binding energy of the reaction intermediates [119]. Furthermore, \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \), which involves the transfer of more than two electrons for the formation of products, multistep proton–electron transfers, and a series of reaction intermediates, significantly complicates \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{R} \) the process. The effects of electronic structure variation on catalyst design and size-dependent mechanisms must be investigated to integrate theoretical and experimental findings holistically. For example, a thorough examination of \( 15\ \mathrm{nm}\ \mathrm{Cu} \) nanoparticle (Cu NP)-based catalysts and bulk foil is required to comprehend the reactivity of bulk and nanoscale materials. Sen et al. recently prepared three-dimensional (3D) \( \mathrm{Cu} \) nanofoam with nanoscale dendritic walls using a facile strategy for its application in \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) [114]. The electrochemical results revealed a significant increase in HER kinetics compared to bulk \( \mathrm{Cu} \), as well as a reduction in \( \mathrm{C}_{1}-\mathrm{C}_{3} \) hydrocarbon to a trace amount. Similarly, Smith et al. investigated the selectivity of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) toward \( \mathrm{C}_{2+} \) hydrocarbons and alcohol over a copper nanowire (NW) array that was adjusted by altering the dimension and density of the NWs [120]. To explain, the
density and length of the NWs can be tuned by varying reaction times. As a result, there is a gradual increase in \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4} \) formation within the length and thickness of \( \mathrm{Cu} \) NWs. At \( 8.1\ \mathrm{mm} \) of \( \mathrm{Cu} \) NW length, the FE of \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4} \) formation is \( 17.4\% \) as \( \mathrm{Cu} \) NW arrays grow longer and denser, resulting in the increased selectivity toward \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4} \) formation while impeding \( \mathrm{H}_{2} \) production. Furthermore, the controlled diffusion of bicarbonate \( \left(\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}\right) \) and hydroxide \( \left(\mathrm{OH}^{-} \right. \)produced) ions into and out of the \( \mathrm{Cu} \) NWs affects the \( \mathrm{pH} \) in the electrode vicinity and thus affects reaction selectivity. In addition, Wang et al. reported \( \mathrm{Cu} \) NW synthesis using a simple two-step method, demonstrating the critical role of \( \mathrm{Cu} \) NW density in \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) [121]. Reske and colleagues also investigated the effect of nano-sized \( \mathrm{Cu} \) in \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) by varying particle size from 2 to \( 15\ \mathrm{nm} \) [116]. According to this study, reducing \( \mathrm{Cu} \) to the nanoscale results in a significant increase in \( \mathrm{H}_{2} \) and \( \mathrm{CO} \) selectivity, as well as a significant reduction in hydrocarbon formation when compared to bulk copper.
Understanding \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) electroreduction over metal nanostructures is difficult because of the combined effects of atomic coordination, grain boundaries, oxidation states, and catalyst dispersion on the activity and selectivity of various reaction products. In comparison to stretched surfaces, the coordination number of nanosurface atoms influences intermediate binding strength and the selectivity of the reaction. Furthermore, a dynamic variation in the selectivity and activity of the \( \mathrm{Cu} \) NP catalyst in \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) adds to the complexity of the reaction. Recently, size-controlled nanoparticles \( (<\ 5\ \mathrm{nm}) \) have been synthesized using a reverse micelle encapsulation method to achieve improved \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) over \( \mathrm{Cu} \) nanoparticles due to low coordinated sites [122, 123]. In this regard, the increased \( \mathrm{H}_{2} \) and \( \mathrm{CO} \) formation on \( \mathrm{Cu} \) NPs is attributed to the small size of NPs and a significant decrease in the selectivity of hydrocarbons when compared to bulk \( \mathrm{Cu} \).
Several other studies have looked into the performance of nano-sized \( \mathrm{Cu} \) catalysts during \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \). The variation in experimental results is linked to the differences in catalyst synthesis methods, nanostructured morphology, and particle size [116, 124]. In some cases, ligands used in synthesis accumulate on the catalyst active sites, affecting reaction performance. Furthermore, \( \mathrm{Cu} \) is naturally nomadic, particularly in the presence of \( \mathrm{CO} \) [125]. The reaction conditions improve significantly depending on the initial geometry, resulting in variations in nanoparticle morphology, size, and dispersion. In this regard, Reske and colleagues demonstrated a nanoparticle (less than \( 15\ \mathrm{nm} \) in diameter) based electrode, with lower selectivity toward hydrocarbon and improved selectivity performance toward \( \mathrm{CO} \) and \( \mathrm{H}_{2} \) when compared to the polycrystalline \( \mathrm{Cu} \) foil (Fig. 6(a)). These findings are consistent with a greater number of low coordination sites on smaller nanoparticles (Figs. 6(b) and 6(c)). Furthermore, a significant increase in current density for nanoparticles \( <\ 5\ \mathrm{nm} \) at a fixed potential indicates an increase in reaction kinetics for different products while leaving particle size unchanged [116].
Large \( \mathrm{Cu} \) NPs, as opposed to small \( \mathrm{Cu} \) NPs, have demonstrated high selectivity toward hydrocarbon [124]. It is critical to investigate the morphological variation of the nanocatalyst during operation to monitor size-dependent trends. As the reaction progresses, \( 7\ \mathrm{nm} \) nanoparticles grow to \( 23\ \mathrm{nm} \) in size, potentially altering selectivity and reaction kinetics (Figs. 6(d) and 6(e)). For instance, Manthiram et al. showed improved methane production over \( 7\ \mathrm{nm} \) \( \mathrm{Cu} \) nanoparticles at overpotentials of \( -0.95 \) and \( -1.45\ \mathrm{V} \) vs. RHE when compared
to polycrystalline Cu [124]. Loiudice et al. fabricated large cube-shaped nanocrystals with sizes ranging from 24 to \( 63\;\mathrm{nm} \). High selectivity toward \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4} \) was observed over \( 44\;\mathrm{nm} \) Cu NPs in this study, with improved FE of \( 80\% \) [74]. The FE of various products over a range of nanoparticle size is shown in Fig. 6(f). It is evident that Manthiram et al. showed an increased particle size from 7 to \( 23\;\mathrm{nm} \) soon after the catalytic process [124]. Furthermore, two of these studies used spherical Cu NPs [124] and the third study used primarily cube-shaped Cu NPs [74]. As previously discussed, variations in NP surface
can affect catalyst activity and selectivity, so different loadings are used during examinations. The unnecessary diversion regarding trends of differently sized Cu nanoparticles complicates the conclusion. Furthermore, the use of the nanocopper surface in catalysis is difficult, and the effect of material dimensions on \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) is not yet clear. Material modeling, in conjunction with in situ analysis, is required to provide in-depth knowledge to better understand these mechanisms. Table 3 shows various Cu-based nanocatalysts and their catalytic efficiency for \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) toward \( \mathrm{C}_{2+} \) products.
## 3.4 Effect of morphology on \(\mathbf{C}_{2+}\) product selectivity
Structure sensitivity, in conjunction with theoretical studies, emphasizes the role of nanomaterials with controlled morphology in evaluating the influence of facets and designing tailored catalysts with superior performance and selectivity. For example, the synthesized cubic-shaped Cu nanostructures have a well-ordered (100) facet with the characteristic of low coordinated sites. These characteristics, together with \(\mathrm{Cu^{+}}\) and subsurface oxygen species, result in increased ethylene selectivity and reduced methane production [132, 139]. The primary disadvantage of shape-selected nanocatalysts is facet loss and variation in particle size during the reaction, which impairs catalyst performance [140]. In this regard, Grosse et al. examined the dynamic morphological transformation of \(100\ \mathrm{nm\Cu}\) cubes with reaction time using operando electrochemical atomic force microscopy (EC-AFM) (Figs. 7(a)–7(e)). As a result, the cubic reformation on air exposure was found to be greater on the corners and edges than on the flat facets. Variations in catalyst size and morphology were observed before and after the reaction, as illustrated in Fig. 7(f). In terms of FE, the selectivity of the ongoing reaction toward \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) and HER was monitored over a variety of time intervals (Fig. 7(g)). The in situ analysis of Cu nanocubes via EC-AFM revealed a decrease in selectivity of ethylene and ethanol with the loss of Cu (100) facet. Larger cubes with modified morphological constancy and a higher ratio of flat surfaces to corner sites and edges promoted \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) kinetics (Fig. 7(g)) [141].
Similarly, Buonsanti et al. studied the degradation mechanism of a nanocatalyst using well-defined and monodispersed copper nanocubes (CuNCs). The schematic representation of the morphological transformation of Cu cubes is shown in Figs. 8(a) and 8(b). It can be seen that the primary degradation during \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) resulted in the formation of \(2{-}4\ \mathrm{nm\Cu}\) nanoclusters [39, 142]. Ex situ stability analysis was performed by collecting CuNCs over specified time intervals from the reaction medium. After \(12\,\mathrm{{h}}\) of reaction time, the morphological evolution showed rapid and precise dispersion of CuNCs into individual cubes with ultimate coherence, and no single CuNCs were left behind (Fig. 8(c)). After \(^{6\,\mathrm{{h}}}\) of reaction, the electrocatalytic performance of CuNCs showed a minor decrease in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) and an increase in HER kinetics. Three-dimensional electron tomography detects the pitting process in CuNCs, which leaves tiny pinholes at the cube's edges. Furthermore, this study supports theoretical findings by demonstrating that the cubic facet interface is the most active site for C–C coupling [143].
