The stability of \(\mathrm{{Cu^{+}}}\) ions on Cu cubes as well as Cu foil proves beneficial in determining reaction selectivity toward \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) . The absence of \(\mathrm{{Cu^{+}}}\) on Cu cubes loaded on glassy carbon, as opposed to Cu foil, shifts \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) reaction selectivity to \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) [144]. The highly porous 3D Cu dendrites and foam exhibit excellent \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) with improved \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) selectivity despite the lack of superior facet reorientation [112]. However, because no superior facet reorientation is observed in these nanostructured materials, the high-density defects (steps, kinks, and edges) [145], grain boundaries [146] and surface roughness [112, 145], and concurrent changes in the interfacial \(\mathrm{pH}\) [147] are regarded as critical considerations outlining the subsequent catalytic behaviors. Furthermore, the duration of the stability of the intermediates inside 3D catalysts is critical for \(C_{2+}\) product selectivity. In this regard, studies have revealed that Cu single-crystal facets, particularly Cu (100) with an optimal step density, exhibit higher selectivity toward C–C bonds compared to other planar surfaces [87, 102]. Enhancement in oxygenating production has also been demonstrated over a variety of crystals, including epitaxially grown Cu films with (751) orientation, as well as Cu (100) and Cu (110) single crystals with terraces and step density [148]. The remarkable performance of single crystals has also piqued the interest of researchers, who have attempted to increase the number of facets in a crystal while retaining its nanomorphology. The performance of face-centered cubic metal Cu (100) has been found to be superior to that of cubic structure. Furthermore, the properties of faceted nanoparticles with edge and corner sites are similar to those of single crystals, with a strong influence on reaction selectivity and performance. Furthermore, DFT studies show that the superior activity of Cu (211) in reducing CO to \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) , as compared to (100) and (111) facets, is linked with a high density of surface step sites [149]. Several research groups have reported the performance of cubic Cu NPs by validating their roles in enhancing \(C_{2+}\) product selectivity over polycrystalline Cu due to (100) facets [115]. For instance, Grosse et al. investigated the drastic loss of the cubic structure of nanoparticles during \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) [141].
Similarly, Wang et al. synthesized cubic Cu nanoparticles using chemical etching spanning over various time intervals to achieve the various shapes shown in Figs. 8(d)–8(g) [150]. The etched nanocrystals for a duration greater than \( 12\,\mathrm{{h}} \) have rhombic dodecahedral morphology with preferential exposure of (110) facets, favoring \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) selectivity toward hydrocarbon with an overpotential of \( -0.8\mathrm{~V~} \) vs. RHE (Figs. 8(h) and 8(i)). However, no liquid product was reported in the studies mentioned above. Later, Hori et al. demonstrated a significant
increase in oxygenate production on single crystals with (110) orientation [151]. An important consideration during the investigation of the morphological effects is the structural stability of Cu nanoparticles for maximizing both performance and morphological integrity, especially when structural transformations are common because of high mobility. For instance, Huang et al. processed cubic Cu nanoparticles with electrolysis time (up to \( 12\;\mathrm{{h}} \) ) for monitoring morphological variations via ex situ analysis using a transmission electron microscopy (TEM). The findings revealed increased HER kinetics, decreased \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \), and imprecision in nanoparticle morphology. Furthermore, structural changes caused by the adsorbate-induced restructuring of the Cu catalyst do not result in catalyst deactivation [152, 153]. Conversely, \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) at \( -1.1\;\mathrm{V} \) vs. RHE is measured, validating nonplanar electrodes in a restricted regime of mass transport and an inactive phase valid at lower overpotentials.
Regardless, there has been little research on nanoparticle faceting, particularly on particle morphology and behavior separately, as opposed to an emphasis on other parameters such as size, loading, and support effects. In this regard, Sargent et al. [48] used in situ soft X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS) and dark-field spectroscopy to track the oxidation state of copper under \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) reduction conditions. Simultaneously, in situ dissolution and redeposition of copper from a sol-gel copper oxychloride have also been observed. These phenomena allow for the formation of various nanostructure shapes from within the bulk material itself.
Initially, the FE of \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4} \) is found to be \( 15\% \), with \( \mathrm{H}_{2} \) as the major product. As the reaction progresses, the change in nanocatalyst morphology causes an increase in \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4} \) production up to \( 54\% \) FE and the FE of \( \mathrm{H}_{2} \) drops down to \( 39\% \), which remains constant afterward. The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images and dark-field spectra recorded before (Fig. 9(a)) and after the reaction (Fig. 9(b)) show a variation in \( \mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}\mathrm{Cl} \) structure. Furthermore, the time-resolved spectroscopy reveals that as the reaction progresses, the large interface between \( \mathrm{Cu}^{+} \)and \( \mathrm{Cu}^{0} \) favors electrostatic attraction between C atoms of \( \mathrm{^{\star}C O@C u^{+}} \) (positively charged) and \( \mathrm{^{\star}C O@C u^{0}} \) (negatively charge), thereby further facilitating the reaction selectivity toward \( C_{2+} \) hydrocarbon (Fig. 9(c)) and suppressing \( \mathrm{CH}_{4} \) formation to less than \( 1\% \) [48]. These findings also indicate that the oxidation state primarily promotes ethylene production, which is consistent with DFT findings. Furthermore, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis detects chlorine on the catalyst surface. The shift in the \( \mathrm{Cu} \) 2p peak indicates a reduction reaction. A sharp chemical shift typical of a copper hydroxide species like \( \mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}\mathrm{Cl} \) is observed prior to the reaction. However, after the reaction, \( \mathrm{Cu} \) \( 2\mathrm{p}_{3/2} \) shows signs of difficulty distinguishing between \( \mathrm{CuO} \) and metallic \( \mathrm{Cu} \) (Figs. 9(d) and 9(e)). Furthermore, together with the XAS measurements, these findings provide evidence for the presence of \( \mathrm{Cu}^{+} \)species in electro-redeposited (ERD) \( \mathrm{Cu} \) during \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \). In this regard, the schematic representation of the catalyst morphology during \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) is shown in Fig. 9(f) [48].
Interparticle spacing is regarded as an important parameter in Cu NP catalysis. To explain, mesoscale phenomena such as interparticle reactant diffusion and intermediate readsorption play an important role in product selectivity in multistep
reactions [155, 156]. For example, Mistry et al. tuned interparticle spacing and tested its effect on \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \). The study revealed that decreasing the interparticle spacing between similar-sized nanoparticles improves \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4} \) and \( \mathrm{CH}_{4} \) selectivity and increases CO readsorption on nearby atoms, thereby assisting with further reduction [157]. These phenomena have the potential to explain the wide variation in selectivity observed in catalysts derived from stretched and scratched Cu surfaces containing highly dispersed Cu nanoparticles on carbon supports. Despite the morphological evolution of nanomaterials in \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) catalysis, there have been few studies published. To that end, Roberts et al. [158] prepared Cu nanocubes on polycrystalline Cu via successive oxidation–reduction cycles to achieve enhanced \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4} \) kinetics. Similarly, Chen and colleagues used the same strategy for nanocube synthesis and improved cyclic life of a catalyst with increased \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4} \) production [139]. The presence of \( \mathrm{Cl}^{-} \)and \( \mathrm{Cu}^{+} \), in addition to Cu (100) facets over a Cu cube, facilitates the
for further investigation. The exposure of different surface sites and changes in interfacial \(\mathrm{pH}\) on rough surfaces facilitate the distribution of \(C_{2+}\) product while suppressing \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) formation. However, the purpose of this work was to investigate the activity and selectivity of \(\mathrm{Cu}\ \mathrm{NP}\) systems via mass transport effects in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\). It is worth noting that the retention time of the reaction intermediates on nanoporous surfaces improves \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) to novel high-energy \(C_{2}\) and \(C_{3}\) hydrocarbons and alcohols.
Large nanosurfaces, as recently established, are advantageous sites for achieving high selectivity toward \(C_{2+}\) products. To explain, large electrochemical surfaces (accessible to electrolyte) promote high currents, facilitate intermediate dimerization, raise \(\mathrm{pH}\) and proton consumption, and discourage \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) evolution [162]. A study involving OD-Cu revealed that surface area is a key factor in limiting the current and overpotential toward \(C_{2+}\) products [163, 164]. Furthermore, owing to the former's normalized surface area, the performance of OD-Cu is comparable to that of polycrystalline Cu foil. Conversely, the differences in the selectivity of two catalysts are primarily attributed to mesoscopic transport effects [165]. In short, a large surface area is a critical factor in material design for improving \(C_{2+}\) product selectivity and tuning the onset potentials for hydrocarbons ( \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) ) and alcohol [112].
## 3.5 Effect of alloying on \(C_{2+}\) product selectivity in Cu catalysis
Furthermore, crystal orientation is useful in determining the product ratio. For example, Cu (100) endorses \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}\) formation, and Cu (111) promotes \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) syntheses [148]. The subsequent steps in \(\mathrm{CO_{2}RR}\) demonstrate that the crystal surface improves reaction selectivity by increasing the binding strength of reactants [140]. In this regard, Hori et al. [148] demonstrated that the surface of Cu (100) steps increases \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}\) selectivity on Cu (711) surface by up to \(58.9\%\). The experimental and theoretical studies are useful in understanding the previously unknown selectivity mechanics on Cu (111) and Cu (100) surfaces. According to the findings, the CO dimerization on the (100) facet facilitates \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}\) production, whereas the protonation of CO over the (111) facet leads to \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) formation [85, 87, 90, 111]. Early experiments show that \(\mathrm{C_{2+}}\) product selectivity is dependent on facet and, and in this regard, even the Cu (100) facet helps in \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}\) formation, the rate of which is two times higher than that of methane on Cu (111) [148]. Further evidence suggests that (100) terraces have the lowest potential barrier to CO dimerization [87, 175], and recent theoretical work shows that dimerization can be modulated further by a charged water layer [90], local field perturbations [176], and high CO coverages [168].
The effect of the alloys of Cu catalysts with other elements has been reported. For instance, when Cu is alloyed with Pt (CuPt NPs), the catalytic reaction fails to operate in the desired manner because of the strong \(\mathrm{Pt-CO}\) bonding and CO-induced segregation over Pt atoms [177]. When compared to disordered alloys, intermetallic NPs are more organized and interrelate more firmly along the stacking direction [178]. In addition, intermetallic NPs offer plausible control over tuning their structure, geometry, and electronic properties, which is helpful in designing a rationale nanocatalyst with enhanced catalytic performance, as shown in Table 4 [179]. These results were confirmed in \(7\ \mathrm{nm}\) intermetallic Au–Cu NPs with an atomic ratio of 1:1 [180]. The X-ray diffraction (XRD) of the Au–Cu NPs, which were made by reducing \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}\) and Au seeds, revealed superlattice peaks that were not present in the disordered face-centered cubic structure. Furthermore, hydrogen and oxygen form weak interactions on the Au surface, making Au–Cu NPs the most feasible Cu alloy for \(\mathrm{CO_{2}RR}\). It is worth noting that increasing the gold content of a Au–Cu alloy promotes CO reaction kinetics while suppressing \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) formation [181].
