In addition, the significance of \(Z\mathfrak{n}\) in the production of CO intermediates has been demonstrated by performing \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) on \(\mathrm{Cu-Ni}\) and \(\mathrm{Cu-Ag}\) bimetallic catalysts. Operando Raman spectroscopy analysis has shown that as the coverage of
Cu-based oxide films is reduced to the metallic state during \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\), only signals corresponding to CO adsorbed on \(\mathrm{Cu}\) sites are recorded. This implies that \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) is more likely to occur on metallic sites rather than on metal oxides. In this regard, a two-site mechanism has been proposed to justify selective \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction to ethanol [187]. Clark et al. [188] synthesized an \(\mathrm{Ag-Cu}\) bimetallic catalyst by incorporating \(\mathrm{Ag}\) atoms at the \(\mathrm{Cu}\) surface, which results in the promotion of an active surface via compressive strain, which is further associated
with the formation of a \(\mathrm{Cu^{+}}\) Ag surface alloy. Surface strain (Fig. 11(a)) increases because of \(\mathrm{Ag}\) atom doping, which can tune the Cu valence bandgap and can reduce the binding energy for H and O as compared to CO. CO's lower binding energy suppresses HER kinetics and promotes the formation of CO-derived products. During \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\), the Cu surface of the Ag-rich bimetallic electrodes is enriched because of CO adsorption, which promotes the segregation of initially dissolved Cu in the Ag phase (Fig. 11(b)). Primarily, CO production initiates across the Ag surface, and further reduction of CO occurs at the Cu surface (Fig. 11(c)). When compared to pure Cu, the bimetallic Ag–Cu electrode can suppress \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) production by up to \(75\%\) [188].
The alloying of gold with Cu is of great interest, and numerous studies have been conducted in this regard. Jaramilo et al. [189], for example, used an \(\mathrm{Au-Cu}\) bimetallic catalyst for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) to demonstrate enhanced selectivity toward alcohol production over other hydrocarbons. The \(\mathrm{Au-Cu}\) bimetallic electrode was created by depositing nanogold on polycrystalline Cu using the physical vapor deposition technique. In terms of performance, the catalytic activity of the synthesized nano- \(\mathrm{Au-Cu}\) alloy differs significantly from those of the original metals and their bimetallic alloys. The mechanism of the \(\mathrm{Au-Cu}\) bimetallic catalyst involves \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction to CO on the Au surface, followed by CO enrichment across the Cu surface. A high concentration of CO at the Cu surface initiates new chemistry of \(C-C\) coupling toward \(C_{2+}\) alcohols at a low overpotential, which has not been previously reported for Cu-based. At higher overpotentials, \(\mathrm{Au-Cu}\) achieves maximum alcohol formation and partial current density (90 mV more positive than at copper), implying that the catalytic role of the \(\mathrm{Au-Cu}\) catalyst is limited by the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) mass transport at lower potentials. At a low overpotential, the rate of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction to \(>2\mathrm{e^{-}}\) products is 100 times greater on \(\mathrm{Au-Cu}\) than on copper (Fig. 11(d)). Current efficiencies and partial current densities determine the selectivity and activity of the \(\mathrm{Au-Cu}\) catalyst toward alcohol formation even at low-overpotential conditions where only gold or copper is feasible (Fig. 11(e)). Consequently, alcohol products are detected at a lower overpotential of \(260\,\mathrm{mV}\) as compared to copper. At high positive potentials, the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction rate is exponentially reduced and only the formation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\), CO, and formate occurs. Furthermore, minor changes in the activity and selectivity of the \(\mathrm{Au-Cu}\) alloy toward gas-phase products over time are significant and are related to the previously mentioned changes in electrode morphology. Another strategy is to combine the novel chemistry of the \(\mathrm{Au-Cu}\) bimetallic electrode and gold NPs, which motivates \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to produce \(>2\mathrm{e^{-}}\) reduction products that are more than two orders of magnitude greater than Cu and Au alone. This increase in the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) kinetics is attributed to the rapid formation of \(C-C\) bonds [189].
The nature of the secondary metal in the bimetallic system has also been found to have a strong influence on reaction selectivity. For instance, Kim et al. explained an increase in CO formation with increasing Au content by optimizing \(\mathrm{Au}_{3}\mathrm{Cu}\) composition, resulting in mass catalytic activity greater than \(200\,\mathrm{A/g}\) at \(-0.73\,\mathrm{V}\) vs. RHE. The difference between Au and \(\mathrm{Au}_{3}\mathrm{Cu}\) toward CO selectivity is not solely due to differences in electronic structure (Fig. 11(f)). Alternatively, the synergetic effect of electronic structure and geometry in \(\mathrm{Au-Cu}\) NPs aids in understanding its dynamic activity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) by tuning the binding energy of reaction intermediates (Fig. 11(g)). The activity trends of \(\mathrm{Au-Cu}\) bimetallic nanoparticles clearly
In the case of metal catalysts, one or more species can be doped for alloying, resulting in a catalyst interface design with multiple sites, and are feasible toward the intermediate binding
pathway [120, 199]. With Cu as the only metal catalyst, the designed catalyst has the tendency to tune selectivity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\), leading to multicarbon products [182]. It is also believed that the catalyst's interfacial engineering can tune the chemical environment at the Cu electrode interface. Several researchers have synthesized nanostructured materials to increase the \(C_{2}/C_{1}\) ratio at a desired current density based on the idea of Cu (100) surfaces favoring \(C_{2+}\) product distribution at an ultralow overpotential. In this regard, strategies such as electrochemical cycling [198], plasma-treated cubes [132], and colloidal synthesized cubes [74] help in optimizing the performance of the Cu electrode interface, and one of the known cubic species formed via in situ reductions is \(\mathrm{CuO}\). Sue et al. investigated the selectivity of desired surfaces using surface treatment/renovation of Cu single nanoscale crystals. For instance, the cube-like \(\left(\mathrm{C-Cu}\right)\), hexahombic decahedron-like \(\left(\mathrm{H-Cu}\right)\), and octahedron-like \(\left(\mathrm{O-Cu}\right)\) Cu nanoscale single crystals are investigated in terms of their potentials for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) (Figs. 13(a)–13(d)). This study demonstrates that hydrocarbon selectivity is highly correlated with surface orientation and electrolyte concentration. In other words, plausible performance in terms of \(\mathrm{H-Cu}\) promoting \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) toward ethanol formation with excellent selectivity of \(25\%\) FE and improving \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) production through the \(\mathrm{C-Cu}\) bond is observed with different
crystal facets. Furthermore, it has been demonstrated that the \(\mathrm{O-Cu}\) crystal facet improves \(\mathrm{CO}\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) reaction kinetics (Figs. 13(e) and 13(f)) [162]. The experimental results also show that the reactivity and selectivity of the crystals in distributing the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) product are directly related to the single-crystal facet.
Advanced electrocatalysis and in situ spectroscopic studies show that the cubic nature and (100) faceting of \(\mathrm{CuO}\) decrease with subsequent reduction during the catalytic process [141]. The catalytic behavior of the aforementioned species remains unknown. However, it can be explained in terms of the involvement of the (100) cubic catalyst facet in tuning the catalytic properties or structural transformation facilitating \(C_{2+}\) product selectivity. For instance, Wang and colleagues investigated the effect of facet-dependent \(\mathrm{C-C}\) bonds on \(\mathrm{Cu}\) catalysts and used a model electrolyte to investigate the solvation and cation stabilization of the initial \(\mathrm{C-C}\) coupling-based intermediates using DFT calculations. The simulation analysis demonstrates the feasibility of \(\mathrm{Cu}\) (100) and stepped facet for \(C_{2+}\) products over \(\mathrm{Cu}\) (111). This explains why \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) nanocubes with a (100) facet produce a sixfold enrichment in \(C_{2+}:C_{1}\) ratio with FE of \(60\%\) [200]. Similarly, Chorkendorf et al. used three different types of \(\mathrm{Cu}\) electrodes: an electropolished surface, a copper nanoparticle-covered surface, and a sputtered
In comparison to Cu nanocubes, rhombic dodecahedral Cu has a positive onset potential of \( -1.1\;\mathrm{V} \) vs. RHE. Furthermore, at \( -1.4\;\mathrm{V} \) vs. RHE, the current density of the rhombic dodecahedral structure is calculated to be nearly three times that of the Cu nanocubes, indicating that this Cu crystal structure with enriched (110) facets yields better catalytic performance than that with a (100) facet. Because of the high-energy facets, the selectivity toward \( \mathrm{CH}_{4} \), \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4} \), \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6} \), and \( \mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8} \) is greater for the (110) facet than the original (100) facet of Cu. These findings suggest that the formation of \( C_{1} \) and \( C_{2} \) is favored on the (110) facet of rhombic dodecahedral Cu [150].
## 3.6 Cu oxide-derived metallic catalysts
\( C_{2+} \) compounds, such as ethylene and ethanol, are more valuable compounds than \( C_{1} \) compounds because of their vast commercial interests [91, 202, 203]. As previously stated, the selectivity for the distribution of the \( C_{2+} \) products can be achieved by manipulating the Cu catalyst design via doping with atoms [204–210], alloying [84, 211–215], introducing defect sites [216], and assembling morphological variations in the catalysts [65, 107, 112, 120, 145, 216, 217]. However, the concept of tuning catalytic selectivity toward \( C_{2+} \) products has yet to mature. Various mechanisms of \( C{-}C \) coupling have been proposed so far to elucidate the catalytic mechanism during consistently repetitive \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) reduction cycles. Kanan and colleagues, for example, prepared OD-Cu using a simple synthesis method, resulting in excellent catalytic performance in terms of high-FE multicarbon products produced during \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) [218]. Numerous studies have previously shed light on the reaction mechanism of the OD-Cu electrocatalyst [219]. For example, \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) using the OD-Cu catalyst has been investigated in in situ conditions using temperature-programmed desorption (TPD) and scanning electrochemical cell microscopy [146, 220–222]. It has been demonstrated that grain boundaries appear as a result of the electrochemical reduction of metal oxide, resulting in a \( C\mathrm{u}^{+} \) defect at the electrode interface and playing a critical role during \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \). Furthermore, Eilert et al. used ambient-pressure XPS to evaluate the performance of OD-Cu and confirmed the presence of oxygen on its subsurface, which aids in increasing the binding energy for CO coupling upon Cu [223]. Table 5 depicts the ground state energy state, whereas Table 6 depicts the adsorbates on three different facets of copper [134] as well as their binding energies and C–C bond energies. Because of the importance of the Cu catalyst in \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \), numerous studies have reported methods to control the oxidation state [48, 224]. For example, Mistry et al. prepared plasma-activated Cu catalysts with \( C\mathrm{u}^{+} \) on the surface for converting \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) to \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4} \) with \( 60\% \) FE [224] Similar
selectivity toward \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) [48]. These findings are validated by performing in situ time-resolved soft XAS on the Cu catalyst to track its oxidation state. It is important to mention that the initial transition of \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) to \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\)is ultrafast whereas further reduction of \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\)species to \(\mathrm{Cu}^{0}\) is much slower. Approximately \(23\%\) of \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\)species remain even after \(1\,\mathrm{h}\) of a constant reduction at \(-1.2\;\mathrm{V}\) vs. RHE (Fig. 14(b)). Additionally, Amal et al. [226] designed 3D heterostructured copper electrode for direct electrochemical \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{R}\) to \(\mathrm{C}_{2+}\) hydrocarbons. The resultant catalyst effaciously reduced \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to ethanol with an excellent FE of \(31\%\) at lowest applied potential of \(-0.3\;\mathrm{V}\). Excellent \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{R}\) performance is attributed to the presence of abundant \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}/\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) interfaces over sandwich Cu electrode. Energy-dispersive X-ray (EDX) data revealed the presence of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{CuO}\) species along the nanowires edges is a potential active sites for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{R}\) [227].
Lee and colleagues recently synthesized a novel \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) catalyst via Cu anodization for demonstrating high stability of favorable \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction to ethylene with FE of \(40\%\) [228]. Despite the importance of the Cu oxidation state in improving catalytic performance, contradictory results regarding OD-Cu catalyst were seen. The catalyst's unusual performance is attributed to the high \(\mathrm{pH}\) in the electrode vicinity, which is caused by a high potential or current [168, 229–233]. Numerous studies have evaluated the OD-Cu catalyst in conjunction with various types of Cu electrodes, and their findings have been supported by DFT calculations [231, 232, 234–237]. Despite the fact that Cu oxidizes quickly in ambient conditions, ex situ analysis has been insufficient to explain the oxidation state [229–231, 236]. As a result, in situ studies have recently become popular for gaining better understanding into their reaction mechanisms. For example, Yeo et al. applied in situ Raman spectroscopy to investigate the relationship between the oxidation state and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) behaviors of Cu, \(\mathrm{CuO}\), and \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) catalysts [230–233]. This research demonstrates that \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) occurs over metallic Cu and does not proceed unless Cu oxide is present on the surface. Later, Li et al. [238] reduced \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to \(\mathrm{C}_{2+}\) hydrocarbon with remarkable FE of \(87\%\) at partial current density of \(217\;\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) over \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\)-D. An extraordinary
potential of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{R}\) to \(\mathrm{C}_{2+}\) hydrocarbon is correlated with Cu facet effect, which not only enhanced CO absorption over catalyst but also facilitated C–C coupling. Furthermore, in situ attenuated total reflectance surface-enhanced infrared absorption spectroscopy (ATR-SEIRAS) and Raman spectra disclose that (110) favored CO adsorption, whereas (100) facilitated C–C coupling to \(\mathrm{C}_{2+}\) products. De Luna and his colleagues also investigated \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) using in situ soft XAS and discovered \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\)at a negative potential. Such in situ studies require sensitive operating conditions to study reaction progress, but such conditions are difficult to obtain [48]. As a result of plasmonic enhancement, Raman spectroscopy has been shown to be more effective than soft XAS. Despite the difficulty of detecting traces of species when observed through an electrolyte, this method operates successfully with insensitivity.
Under normal conditions, in situ soft XAS shows tremendous advancement when it comes to monitoring the oxidation states of metals [48, 239], but this technique is unable to detect the reaction intermediates of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\). This problem can thus be solved using in situ ATR-SEIRAS [240–246]. Because of the infrared radiation beam that can pass through a metal prism placed on the backside of the working electrode, this technique has a significant advantage in terms of investigating the chemical species at the electrode surface. To evaluate \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) products, this analysis can also be performed under the same conditions as those in controlled potential electrolysis. ATR-SEIRAS and Raman spectroscopy can be used in tandem to monitor the changes in the oxidation state of the Cu electrode [242, 243]. When the in situ ATR-SEIRAS study is used to elucidate the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) mechanism, the results explain not only the adsorption site position and morphology but also the contribution of chemical species like bicarbonate and hydrated cations. For instance, Masuda et al. used the in situ ATR-SEIRAS technique to demonstrate that increasing the concentration of several \(-\mathrm{OH}\) groups on the catalyst surface significantly increases CO adsorption, which leads to an increase in CO–CO dimerization and favors the formation of \(\mathrm{C}_{2+}\) products [247]. Furthermore, spectroscopic investigations reveal a lower concentration of \(\mathrm{CO}\) intermediates on the surface \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) electrode (Fig. 14(c)), in contrast to other Cu electrodes with an OH layer \((\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}/\mathrm{Cu})\) on the Cu surface (Fig. 14(d)). The \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}/\mathrm{Cu}\) catalyst has high CO concentration and enhanced \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction activity. In addition, the OH layer prevents the Cu electrode from deactivating by forming a top bridge confined to the metallic Cu surface (Fig. 14(d)), thereby prompting CO molecules to come closer at minimal distances and reduce to \(\mathrm{C}_{2+}\) hydrocarbons [45]. The reaction performance of various oxide-derived Cu catalysts under different physiological conditions is displayed in Table 7.
Similarly, Zhang et al. proposed annealing \(\mathrm{CuO}\) micropore nanowire (\(\mathrm{CuO}\) MPNWS) at \(180\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to produce oxygen-bearing copper (\(\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{O}.\mathrm{OBC}\)), as shown in Fig. 15(a). At a partial current density of \(44.7\;\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) in a neutral electrolyte, the prepared hierarchical pore and nanograin boundary of the \(\mathrm{OBC}(\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{O})\) (Fig. 15(b) before \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) and Fig. 15(c) after \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\)) catalyst display catalytic selectivity of \(45\%\) toward \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) formation. The outstanding \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) selectivity of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{O}.\mathrm{OBC}\) attributed to the presence of oxygen atoms over the catalyst surface promotes CO adsorption, dimerization, and advanced C–C coupling reaction as shown in Figs. 15(d)–15(f) [248]. Casadevall et al. investigated the microstructure and thermodynamic binding energy of the adsorbate in another study [222]. Upon carrying out TEM nanodiffraction, the data reveal the irregular shape of \(\mathrm{OD-Cu}\), which is made up of
\(100{-}300\ \mathrm{nm}\) grains joined together via grain boundaries (GBs) in a dense polycrystalline network (Figs. 15(g) and 15(h)). Furthermore, the TPD technique is used to examine the binding strength of CO. The OD-Cu produced by oxidation at a high temperature of \(500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) exhibits high-temperature characteristics at \(275\;\mathrm{~K~}\) that are not present in regular polycrystalline Cu foil (Figs. 15(i) and 15(j)). As a result, microkinetic modeling demonstrates that the binding of CO over the surface of Cu via specific binding sites and porosities is attributed to the presence of a strong CO binding site with a binding energy of \(67\,\mathrm{kJ/mol}\).
Furthermore, Sargent et al. showed that \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) ions present near the Cu interface lower the activation energy barrier for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) and CO coupling. At a low overpotential of \(-0.165\:\mathrm{V}\) vs. RHE, lowering the activation energy enhances \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) production in the alkaline electrolyte and CO formation with \(70\%\) FE. Theoretical studies also validated the experimental results by assessing the effect of hydroxide ions on the thermodynamic and activation energy barriers of CO dimerization.
The DFT calculations performed to consider Cu (111), (100), and (110) surfaces revealed that the hydroxide ions over the Cu surface can reduce the binding energy of CO (Fig. 16(a)). Furthermore, hydroxide species contribute to the charge imbalance between CO–CO adsorbed carbon atoms, which further stabilizes the reaction intermediate via dipole–dipole interactions within the CO–CO system (Figs. 16(b)–16(e)). The combination of all of these effects lowers the energy barrier by \(156~\mathrm{meV}\) on Cu (100), along with an \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) surface accommodating two \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) ions per \(16\,\mathrm{Cu}\) atoms. Such effect of the hydroxide ion on the ethylene formation along Cu (100) and Cu (111) is illustrated in Figs. 16(f) and 16(g) [134]. Through dramatically altering the diffusion kinetics to ensure a high local concentration of \(C_{1}\) intermediates for carbon dimerization, nanoconfinement offers a promising solution for endorsing electrocatalytic C–C coupling. Xia et al. [249] used finite element approach to unveil \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) potential of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) hollow multi-shell structures (HoMSs) with tunable shell numbers. In situ fabricated Cu HoMSs show direct correlation between shell numbers and selectivity towards \(C_{2+}\) products. The higher the degree of shell number within HoMSs, the
greater promotion of CO absorption within nanovoids and hence \(C{-}C\) coupling. Wang et al. investigated the effect of the Cu oxidation state on \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) using electron microscopy and electron energy loss spectroscopy of two Cu electrodes (HQ-Cu and AN-Cu) with different oxidation states. Specifically, the HQ-Cu electrode is made of Cu and \({\mathrm{CuO}},\) whereas the AN-Cu electrode contains Cu and \({\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) ; both are pretreated to \({\mathrm{Cu}}^{0}\) for enhancing the distribution of the \(C_{2+}\) hydrocarbon when compared with their unpretreated counterparts. As a result, the oxide/hydroxide crystals present in HQ-Cu and AN-Cu are fragmented into nano-sized irregular Cu grains, which is not the case with electropolished Cu. The fragmentation process is critical for establishing a complex network of GBs and exposing a variety of high-index facets, facilitating the \(C{-}C\) bond and increasing \(C_{2+}\) selectivity [33]. Similarly, using HPR-layer double hydroxide (LDH), HPR-LDH- \(\cdot2\mathrm{~h~}\) , and HPR-LDH- \(4\,\mathrm{~h~}\) , a \({\mathrm{Cu-Cu}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}\) catalyst prepared by the electrochemical treatment of Cu containing LDHs has been investigated for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) . In particular, HPR-LDH facilitates the formation of a \(C{-}C\) bond and synthesis of long-term \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) over HPR-LDH-2 h and HPR-LDH-4 h, the phenomena further related to the high subsurface of oxides. Furthermore, ex situ surface studies conducted via XRD, SEM, electron spin resonance, and XPS show that HPR-LDH has a remarkable performance toward \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) formation. This performance is explained by the high oxygen content and generation of surface oxygen vacancies in the catalyst surface in the form of mixed oxides (e.g., \({\mathrm{Cu-Cu}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}})\) [250].
The initial thickness of the oxide film is also crucial to assisting the selectivity of oxide-derived Cu catalysts. To explain this, Koper et al. [251] investigated the effect of increasing the initial oxide film thickness. In comparison to \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}\) selectivity, an inverse relation between \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) selectivity and oxide film thickness is observed during testing. Later, Yeo et al. [71] attempted to demonstrate the parabolic dependence of \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\)
selectivity on initial oxide film thickness ranging from 0.2 to \(8.8~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) . In this study, it was discovered that moderate film thicknesses exhibit the highest \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) formation with FE of \(40\%\) . In the abovementioned two studies, \(C\mathrm{H}_{4}\) selectivity is decreased with increasing oxide thickness. In a similar study, Kanan and colleagues [218] prepared oxide-derived Cu through thermal annealing and reported high FE of \(45\%\) for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) at a low overpotential of \(-0.3\;\mathrm{V}\) vs. RHE and lowering the formation of hydrocarbon at a high overpotential. The excellent performance of \(\mathrm{OD-Cu}\) in this regard is attributed to the GBs, which stabilize the \(C{-}O\) bond on the catalyst surface and improve CO reduction toward hydrocarbon [222]. In addition, other than the characteristic reactivity of \(\mathrm{OD-Cu}\) , another mechanism involves \(C\mathbf{u}^{+}\) sites that remain on the catalyst surface. This has been validated by the ex situ Auger measurements conducted by Kim et al. [252] Molecular catalysts immobilized on surfaces have several significant advantages over heterogeneous catalysts, including a controllable 3D structure and a high electron transfer rate. Han and coworker [253] fabricated 3D dendritic copper-cuprous oxide composite via in situ reduction of an electrodeposited copper complex. As-prepared \(\mathrm{Cu-Cu_{2}O/C u}\) electrode exhibited novel characteristics of dendritic 3D structure and near zero contact resistance amid the catalyst and the substrate and displayed exceptional FE of \(80\%\) towards \(C_{2+}\) products in KCl aqueous solution. Decreasing contact resistance between catalyst and substrate proves advantageous in exposing catalyst active sites and heightened reaction performance. In summary, the \(\mathrm{OD-Cu}\) catalyst exhibits promising catalytic performance in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) , but the mechanism explaining the detailed step-by-step evolution of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) on the \(\mathrm{OD-Cu}\) surface remains unknown. As a result, in situ and operando studies are urgently required to uncover the mechanism of the \(\mathrm{OD-Cu}\) catalyst and to gain thorough understanding of the chemical species generated during the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{R}\) reaction.
## 3.7 Metal–organic frameworks for \(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\mathbf{RR}\)
3.7 Metal–organic frameworks for \(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\mathbf{RR}\)Previously, zeolites, porous carbons, porous organic polymers, and MOFs containing ILs were widely used for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) to aid in the formation of valuable chemicals. Figure 17(a) shows a typical Cu-MOF structure for the electrocatalytic reduction of \(C O_{2}\) to valuable hydrocarbons [264–266]. MOFs have emerged as a new class of porous materials constructed via atomic level periodicity of organic ligands and metal ions (Fig. 17(b)) [267, 268]. MOFs' distinct physical and chemical properties are due to their uniform distribution. MOFs with such uniform distribution have novel chemical properties and promising prospects for forming polycrystalline materials with a unified network and abundant interfaces [269]. MOFs' highly desirable properties, such as a large surface area with high porosity, configurable pore size, tunable chemical functionality, and exceptional chemical stability, have resulted in a plethora of energy and environmental applications, explaining why MOFs have become a hot topic [270, 271]. A variety of MOFs have been employed in electrocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) [272]. For instance, cyclic voltammetry investigations have revealed that the \({\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{I})\) based MOFs are highly selective in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) for forming \(C_{2+}\) hydrocarbons [273]. Methods such as pyrolysis at programmable temperatures bestow MOFs with thermodynamic robustness and make them excellent conductors, resulting in materials with exceptional electrocatalytic properties. The adjustable molecular structures and atomic level periodicity of Cu complexes also aid in the creation of active sites in Cu-based MOFs, making them a potential catalyst for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) [274–278].
Multifunction-coupled Cu MOFs can be synthesized by tailoring \({\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) to \({\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}@{\mathrm{Cu}}\) -MOF via time-resolved controllable restructuration. The restructured \({\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}@{\mathrm{Cu}}\) -MOF has a precise surface area with durable adsorption capability of \(C O_{2}\) and prolific active sites for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) . However, when tested, the \({\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}@{\mathrm{Cu}}\) -MOF catalyst has high FE of \(63.2\%\) for \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) and low FE of \(17\%\) for ethylene (Fig. 17(c)) [279]. For instance, Zhao et al. [280] designed oxide-derived Cu/carbon \(\mathrm{(OD-Cu/C)}\) catalysts for \(C O_{2}\) reduction via carbonization of Cu-based MOF (Cu-HKUST-1) with metallic content controlled by pyrolysis temperature. With total FE of \(45.2\%{-}71.2\%\) at \(-0.1\) to \(-0.7\mathrm{~V~}\) vs. RHE, Cu-HKUST-1 exhibits high selectivity for \(C O_{2}\) reduction to \(C_{2+}\) alcohol. Remarkably, the start potential for \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{OH}\) formation is calculated to be approximately \(0.1\;\mathrm{V}\) vs. RHE, which is comparable to the overpotential of \(190\;\mathrm{mV},\) the ultralow achieved so far. However, a high temperature causes an increase in the size of copper nanocrystals as well as the uncontrolled conglomeration of large metal atom clusters. The SEM images of Cu-HKUST-1, shown in Fig. 17(d), reveal that the aggregates destroy MOF porosity, resulting in decreased catalytic performance [280]. Growing MOF nanocrystals within mesoporous carbons with enhanced dispersion between pyrolysis metal clusters can address this issue [281]. Porous MOFs can also be built with the coordination of organic ligands and metal ions for engrossed eye-catching chemistry [282]. Soukri et al. [283] carbonized commercially synthesized MOFs HKUST-1 and PCN-62 over Cu at pyrolysis temperatures ranging from 400 to \(800\ ^{\circ}C\) . The resulting MOFs can be used to reduce \(C O_{2}\) to isopropanol, a previously unknown product, at a constant potential of \(-2.0\) to \(-2.5\mathrm{~V~}\) vs. RHE. The carbonized Cu-HKUST-1 and Cu-PCN-62 operate at a voltage less than \(1.6\,\mathrm{~V~}\) vs. RHE with FE of \(72\%\) , which is higher as compared to that of uncoated Cu. Similarly, Albo et al. [284] used the following four Cu-MOF porous materials:
(1) HKUST-1 MOF \([\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\upmu_{6}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}_{9}\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{6})_{2}]_{n},\) (2) CuAdeAce MOF \([\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\upmu_{3}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{N}_{5})_{2}]_{n},\) (3) CuDTA mesoporous metal-organic aerogel (MOA) \([\mathrm{Cu}(\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2})]_{n},\) and (4) CuZnDTA MOA. For electrocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) , \([\mathrm{Cu}_{0.6}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.4}(\mathrm{u}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2})]_{n}\) is sustained on gas diffusion electrodes (GDE). Surface analysis and voltammetric investigation show that HKUST-1 has a large surface area with utmost porosity and high conductance. At \(1.0\;\mathrm{V}\) vs. \(\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}\) , HKUST-1 begins \(C O_{2}\) electroreduction, followed by a six-electron pathway to generate methanol and ethanol via a 12-electron mechanism. Meanwhile, FTIR and XRD studies demonstrate that a \(5\;\mathrm{h}\) reaction in GDE results in a continuous activity loss and FE dropping to \(65\%\) , \(98\%\) , and \(51\%\) for CuAdeAce, CuDTA, and CuZnDTA, respectively. Conversely, HKUST-1 exhibits continuous formation rates up to \(17\,\mathrm{h},\) which is attributed to the preservation of the MOF structure despite a significant loss in crystallinity over the reaction time.
In conclusion, the lack of understanding of the catalytic reaction mechanisms, low reduction potential, ultrasmall selectivity toward specific products, and insufficient long-term stability are major shortcomings that must be addressed before these Cu-based MOF electrocatalysts can be commercialized. As a result, developing appropriate Cu-MOF nanocatalysts and studying their structural evolution can be critical in gaining further insight into MOF-based catalysts with high selectivity for \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) for achieving commercially important hydrocarbons.
## 3.8 SACs
Because of their excellent surface exposure, SACs are a new class of emerging materials in the field of electrocatalytic \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) reduction. Because of the high surface energy of the intermediates on SACs, incomplete electron transfer occurs, resulting in CO along with limited formation of \( \mathrm{C}_{2+} \) hydrocarbons [289]. Furthermore, the formation of unwanted products on the cathode continues to be a barrier to the commercialization of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) [290, 291]. In general, SACs form a uniform coordinating environment with novel electronic properties of metal centers and exhibit exceptional selectivity in numerous catalytic processes [292–296]. Numerous studies have been conducted to investigate SACs' potential to enhance the electrocatalytic reduction process and improve product selectivity [297, 298]. Despite significant progress, previous research has been limited to single-electron reduction products such as CO and HCOOH due to archaic manipulation in single-atom structures [299]. For example, the synthesis of metal-nitrogen-carbon compounds (M-N-Cs) is carried out through redispersion of aggregated metal nanoparticles in-between micron-sized carbon substrates at high temperatures [300, 301]. Because of
harb reaction conditions, the N- and C-precursors lose their original structures, combine with the metal atom, and result in the thermodynamically stable \( \mathrm{MN}_{x}\mathrm{C}_{4-x} \) moiety [302, 303]. As a result, during thermal pyrolysis, different precursors are unable to tune the coordination structures and thus control the inherent properties of the designed SACs [304]. Figure 18(a) depicts the general synthesis strategy for SACs.
The presence of the mono-atomic active site, which can only stabilize \( ^{\ast}\mathrm{CO} \) species and thus limit the possibility of \( \mathrm{C}_{2+} \) product formation, is generally attributed to the selectivity of SACs toward the \( \mathrm{C}_{1} \) product. However, designing a SAC surface with a molecular framework opens up a new pathway for the formation of \( \mathrm{C}_{2+} \) products by providing a second active site for the C–C bond [292]. To explain, loading metals on \( \mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4} \) results in high metal loading and appropriate coordination capability, which can then be used as a high-performance \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) electrocatalyst [305]. High metal loading and appropriate coordination capability may be attained by loading metals on \( \mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4} \), which can be used as desirable \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) electrocatalysts with auspicious performance [306, 307]. Previously, \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{OH} \) synthesis has been reported on Cu and C active sites, but studies have failed to systemically study their operational dynamics due to the complex reaction mechanisms over distinct active sites. As a result of the lack of experimental evidence regarding \( \mathrm{Cu/C} \) reaction chemistry, it is still unclear whether \( \mathrm{Cu/C} \) is the optimal combination, which limits our understanding of the problem [308]. However, this problem has been fixed by in-depth analysis of mono-atomic Cu with dual active sites loaded on \( \mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4} \). In this regard, Jiao et al. investigated extensive distinct C–C coupling pathways on \( \mathrm{Cu-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4} \) active sites with different combinations of Cu, C, and N active sites and discovered that \( \mathrm{Cu/C} \) provides stability
to the coupling intermediates while keeping the binding energy at a moderate level (Fig. 18(b)). The eccentricity of \(g\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) as a molecular template for SACs, however, provides an additional active center for the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) according to the study. This more active center encourages \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) toward \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) or other possible \(C_{2+}\) species [308]. Similarly, Guan et al. used a temperature-tuned N-coordination strategy to create single-atom Cu catalysts \(\left(\mathrm{Cu-N}_{x}\right)\) with different Cu doping concentrations. The temperature-tuned N-coordination strategy has been shown to be useful in adjusting the distance between adjacent \(\mathrm{Cu-N}_{x}\) sites [309]. In addition, a theoretical study shows that two adjacent \(\mathrm{Cu-N}_{x}\) sites in a \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}@\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{N}\) complex display high selectivity toward \(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) at a low limiting potential of \(-0.76\;\mathrm{V}\) vs. RHE, whereas single \(\mathrm{Cu-N}_{x}\) species favor \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) formation [310, 311].
Furthermore, theoretical studies were conducted while three metal atoms were embroidered on nitrogen-doped porous carbon to demonstrate remarkable catalytic activity and selectivity for \(C_{2}-C_{3}\) hydrocarbons [312, 313]. The \(\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\) clusters anchored on the \(g\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) monolayer exhibit mixed charge states of \(C\mathbf{u}^{0}\) and \(C\mathbf{u}^{x+}\) [313]. Furthermore, by delivering additional electrons, the \(C\mathbf{u}^{0}\) site provides a platform for the adsorption of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{R}\) species, whereas \(C\mathbf{u}^{x+}\) species provide extra adsorption sites for the CO dimer. As a result, CO dimers on adjacent \(C\mathbf{u}^{x+}\) sites form \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{OH}\) with increased selectivity at a low limiting potential of \(-0.68\,\mathrm{~V~}\) vs. RHE. Chen et al. created copper-encapsulated N-doped porous carbon (Cu-SA/NPC) SACs for the selective reduction of \(C\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to \(C_{2+}\) products in another study. The SEM image of the synthesized Cu-SA/NPC SAC composite is displayed in Fig. 18(c). During \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\), it was discovered that acetone is the major \(C_{2}\) product reported, with FE of \(36.7\%\) along with a \(336.1~\upmu\mathrm{g/h}\) production rate. Furthermore, the DFT calculations reveal the anchoring of the central copper atom with four pyrrole-N atoms, which becomes the primary reaction center, resulting in a lower free Gibbs energy for \(C\mathrm{O}_{2}\) activation and increased C–C coupling. The reaction mechanics of selective acetone formation over Cu-SA/NPC have also been
proposed, as well as the thermodynamically favored paths for \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COCH}_{3}\) synthesis (Fig. 18(d)) [312].
Despite extensive efforts to understand and improve the role of SACs in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\), fine-tuning SAC coordination for accurately altering catalytic selectivity and yielding multiple electron-reducing products has remained a significant challenge. Furthermore, the electrocatalytic activity of Cu SACs results in \(C_{1}\) products due to the lack of adsorption sites for multi-CO dimers. Recently, the concept of an advanced multiatom site for tuning \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) selectivity toward \(C_{2+}\) products has been proposed. More research is needed, however, to further illuminate the Cu single-atom catalyst using high-resolution in situ spectroscopy to gain better understanding of the reaction mechanics of SACs and gain more control over tuning the selectivity to multielectron \(C_{2+}\) products.
## 3.9 Effect of interface
The manipulation of the electronic structure and morphology of Cu-based catalysts has been shown to be effective in adjusting the adsorption of H, COOH, and CO intermediates on the catalyst surface, which promotes the C–C bond [127, 314–318]. In this regard, Sargent et al. [204] improved the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) kinetics for assisting \(C_{2}\) product formation by tuning the Cu electronic structure with a boron atom. Fine-tuning interaction between the CO intermediate and Cu catalyst by adjusting the \(\mathrm{Cu}^{4+}\)-to-\(\mathrm{Cu}^{0}\) ratio on the catalyst surface promotes C–C coupling. Similarly, Bell et al. [188] created a \(\mathrm{Cu-Ag}\) alloy on the surface of a Cu substrate to tune the Cu valence band, which aids in further suppressing HER kinetics and increasing \(C_{2+}\) product selectivity. Furthermore, excessive \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) accumulation at the catalyst interface is attributed to uncontrolled \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) mass transport in flowing gas systems. Owing to the strong adjacent interactions between adsorbed \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and reaction intermediates, the lack of active sites reduces \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) formation [319]. In general, the electronic structure of metallic Cu can be modified through doping [84, 164] or molecular-modification [320, 321], and such modification
ensures a promising increase in ethylene formation, i.e., \(64\%\) in an membrane electrode assembly (MEA) electrolyzer [320]. However, designing a catalyst with fast \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) kinetics, effective and uninterrupted electron/proton transfer steps, and excellent control over \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{C}\) coupling with \(60\%\) ethylene efficiency and activity \(>300\;\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) is quite difficult [237].
with their reaction energies. As a result, when compared to the parent catalyst, the viability of \(\mathrm{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}\) bond formation between these intermediates and succeeding reaction pathways is greatly increased, paving the way toward the \(C_{2+}\) hydrocarbon. The resultant catalyst displays \(\sim63\%\) FE toward \(C_{2}\) oxygenates at a potential of \(-0.5\;\mathrm{V}\) vs. RHE. Furthermore, this catalyst exhibits unprecedented persistent catalytic performance for up to \(120\,\mathrm{h}\), with a constant current and only \(19\%\) activity decay [323]. Inspired by natural enzyme-catalyzed reactions, Wang et al. created an artificial chemical environment over the \(\mathrm{Cu}\) surface to activate \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and enrich the catalyst surface with \(\mathrm{CO}\) intermediates while also making the surface more susceptible to \(\mathrm{CO}-\mathrm{CO}\) coupling toward \(C_{2}\) hydrocarbons. During the designing of a nitrogen-doped carbon \((\mathrm{N}_{x}\mathrm{C})\) functional shell on the surface of \(\mathrm{Cu}\) NPs, the \(\mathrm{N}\) species in \(\mathrm{N}_{x}\mathrm{C}\) located at the surface of the \(\mathrm{Cu/C}\) interface play an important role in enriching and triggering \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\), resulting in bond rearrangement up on the \(\mathrm{Cu}\) surface. A schematic of the \(\mathrm{Cu}\) surface covered with a
porous \(\mathrm{N}_{x}\mathrm{C}\) layer is shown in Fig. 19(a). Furthermore, during catalytic operation, the \(\mathrm{Cu/N_{x}C}\) interface-enriched \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) exhibits FE greater than \(90\%\) , with \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{OH}\) as major products with FE greater than \(80\%\) (Fig. 19(b)). In addition to providing excellent electrocatalytic performance, the \(\mathrm{N}_{x}\mathrm{C}\) overlayer stabilizes the \(\mathrm{Cu}\) substrate by inhibiting morphological variations [46].
## 3.10 Polymer-incorporated Cu electrodes
Unlike previous studies, Andreoli et al. [207] used metallic electrocatalysts with polymers to demonstrate a significant
increase in hydrocarbon formation. A primary motif for using polyamine with \(\mathrm{Cu}\) is copper carbamate combined with amine. Furthermore, the excellent interactions between amine- \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules reduce \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) activation energy (Fig. 20(a)) [333, 334]. However, coating electrodeposited copper foam with poly(acrylamide) results in a twofold increase in the \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) formation. Aside from poly(acrylamide), poly(acrylic acid) has similar but transitory catalyst activity, whereas poly(allylamine) inhibits \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) completely (Fig. 20(b)). DFT calculations show that poly(acrylamide) adsorbed on the copper surface via the oxygen atom on the carbonyl groups that boosts ethylene formation by donating charge to the copper surface. This charge exchange activates CO dimerization and provides chemical stabilization to the CO dimer, which is an important intermediate in the formation of \(C_{2}\) products. Furthermore, Cu-poly(acrylamide) has multiple sites that are responsible for developing interactions during the activation and stabilization of the intermediate, resulting in increased ethylene formation (Figs. 20(c) and 20(d)). In addition to incorporating \(\mathrm{Cu}\) with poly(acrylamide), the \(\mathrm{Cu}\) (100) facet and porosity in \(\mathrm{Cu}\) catalyst increase the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) mass transport in a porous structure and favor \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) formation. However, enriching multiphase electron transport reactions on catalysts and developing nonoptimal relationships among reaction intermediates have serious limitations. Nevertheless, multipoint binding sites, such as enzymes with 3D active sites, are expected in next-generation heterogeneous \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) catalysts. Gewirth et al. [335] used a stable amine-containing additive to coat \(\mathrm{Cu}\) electrodes with varying degrees of methylation in this regard. The schematic of synthesizing a polymer-Cu catalyst is shown in Fig. 20(e). This operando Raman investigation reveals that at higher electrode \(\mathrm{pH}\), the \(\mathrm{CO}\) concentration and stabilized intermediate species ratio are higher on the \(\mathrm{Cu}\)-polyamine electrode rather than on the \(\mathrm{Cu}\) surface (Fig. 20(f)). In other words, at high electrode \(\mathrm{pH}\), it becomes easier to tune the \(\mathrm{Cu}\)-polyamine catalyst toward ethylene formation by enriching \(\mathrm{CO}\) and stabilizing the intermediate by enhancing their interplay. The FE for \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) formation during operation is calculated to be \(72\%\) at \(-0.97\,\mathrm{V}\) vs. RHE with a partial ethylene current density of \(312\mathrm{~mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) in \(1\,\mathrm{M}\,\mathrm{KOH}\). Increasing the electrolyte concentration \((1{-}10\,\mathrm{M}\,\mathrm{KOH})\) has also been shown to improve the \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) formation by up to \(87\%\) at \(-0.47\,\mathrm{V}\) vs. RHE, which is equivalent to \(50\%\) FE enhancement in full-cell at a cell potential of \(2.02\,\mathrm{V}\) (Figs. 20(g) and 20(h)). So far, few studies on the incorporation of polymers on the surface of \(\mathrm{Cu}\) electrodes and tuning the reaction selectivity toward \(\mathrm{Cu}\) hydrocarbons have been published. In conclusion, syncing functional moiety can change catalytic kinetics and can provide an additional control for developing \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) catalyst designs.
## 4 Effect of electrolyte and \(\mathrm{pH}\) on \(\mathrm{C}_{2+}\) selectivity
The importance of electrolytes in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) cannot be overstated. Therefore, understanding their unique reaction mechanism through electrolyte design to deliver control over reactivity and selectivity of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) is critical. In general, the performance of an electrolyte is heavily dependent on the size of the cations to facilitate the distribution of \(\mathrm{C}_{2+}\) products on the catalyst interface. The schematic representation of the effect of alkali metal ions and bicarbonate on \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) is shown in Fig. 21(a). Large-sized alkali metal cations \((\mathrm{K}^{+}\)and \(\mathrm{Cs}^{+})\) clearly have a significant influence on \(\mathrm{C}_{2+}\) product selectivity, regardless of the catalytic nature of \(\mathrm{Cu}\) or \(\mathrm{CuO}_{x}\) [206] (Fig. 21(b)). Singh
et al. [336], for example, provided a theoretical explanation of the effect of cation hydrolysis on the electrode–electrolyte interface. A strong buffering effect has been established, which may reduce \( \mathrm{pH} \) in the electrode vicinity, resulting in a high \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) concentration. The in situ SEIRAS and operando Raman spectroscopy have also been employed for investigating the \( \mathrm{pH} \) at the electrode–electrolyte interface [337]. Furthermore, theoretical results have revealed intermediate species that aid in the formation of stabilized \( \mathrm{C}_{2+} \) products at the outer Helmholtz plane (OHP). \( \mathrm{C}_{2+} \) is stabilized by electrostatic interactions between solvated cations and large dipole moments of adsorbed species (Fig. 21(c)) [338]. Furthermore, the cations colonize only at the OHP on the metallic \( \mathrm{Cu} \) surface in this study [339]. Gao et al. [206] recently demonstrated the precise adsorption of solvated cations at both metallic and preoxidized \( \mathrm{Cu} \) electrode surfaces during the \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) process in another study. According to the DFT calculations, surface oxygen facilitates the adsorption of selective cation and
accentuates the function of the cations in the enhancement of \( \mathrm{CO^{*}} \) intermediate formation (Fig. 21(d)).
Multivalent cations, in addition to alkali metal cations, can improve \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) kinetics and promote \( \mathrm{C}_{2+} \) product selectivity. On a \( \mathrm{CuSnPb} \) electrode [340], for example, the \( \mathrm{La}^{3+} \) cations accelerate the rate of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) by two orders of magnitude when compared to that of \( \mathrm{Na}^{+} \)cations in the acidic electrolyte at the same potential. Furthermore, the presence of \( \mathrm{Zr}^{4+} \) species promotes ethanol production with FE of \( 17.6\% \), whereas \( \mathrm{Na}^{+} \)cations facilitate \( \mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH} \) and \( \mathrm{HCOOH} \) formation with FEs of \( 35\% \) and \( 28\% \), respectively. However, the multivalent cation's low solubility in aqueous media limits their practical applications. Furthermore, bicarbonate salts are commonly used as the electrolyte in \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \). According to operando FTIR spectroscopy, the rapid equilibrium generated between the bicarbonate cation and dissolved \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) in solution increases the \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) concentration at the electrode–electrolyte interface [240]. However, the bulk \( \mathrm{pH} \) differs from that in the interface. In addition, proton
consumption or the release of \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) is linked with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) and HER, both causing \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) concentration gradient, resulting in \(\mathrm{pH}\) escalation in the electrode vicinity. The change in local \(\mathrm{pH}\) during the reaction is determined by several variables, including the electrolyte's bulk \(\mathrm{pH}\), buffer capacity, current density, and diffusion layer thickness. Furthermore, combining halide with a bicarbonate electrolyte results in a plausible increase in \(C_{2+}\) product formation when compared to plasma-oxidized \(\mathrm{Cu}\). This increase is also attributed to the formation of a thicker halide layer on the preoxidized \(\mathrm{Cu}\) surface [206]. In contrast to previous research, the performance of a \(\mathrm{Cu}\) (100) single-crystal facet [341] in pure potassium halide electrolytes is evaluated, and an increase in ethylene and ethanol synthesis is observed from \(\mathrm{Cl^{-}}\) to \(\mathrm{Br^{-}}\) to \(\mathrm{I^{-}}\). However, Koper et al. [98] used in situ FTIR spectroscopy to investigate the activity of the \(\mathrm{CO}\) dimer (OCCOH) on the \(\mathrm{Cu}\) (100) electrode in an LiOH solution at low overpotentials. This study offers the simplest strategy for confirming the electrocatalysis of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction on copper and \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{C}\) coupling to \(C_{2}\) species on the \(\mathrm{Cu}\) (100) surface through reductive dimerization.
Conversely, the vibrational features corresponding to the \(\mathrm{CO}\) dimer cannot be observed on the \(\mathrm{Cu}\) (111) surface under identical conditions, confirming that \(\mathrm{CO}\) dimerization is a structure-sensitive process aided by sites with square symmetry. An overview of typical spectroelectrochemical techniques utilized for understanding \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) is illustrated in Fig. 21(e). Through strong binding with the adsorbed species, it can be seen that the adsorbed halides across the \(\mathrm{Cu}\) metal participate in early \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) activation and promote the hydrogenation of the reaction intermediates [341, 342]. Furthermore, the ability to control HER kinetics by adjusting the \(\mathrm{pH}\) of the electrolyte has been demonstrated. High \(\mathrm{pH}\) has been found to reduce HER kinetics and to facilitate ethylene formation over methane [343]. The acidic behavior of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) complicates achieving strong alkaline electrolytes in an H-type electrochemical cell [344].
The cell GDE configured (Fig. 21(f)) in conjunction with concentrated KOH to accomplish strong alkalinity provides the advantage of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) diffusion across the gas–liquid interface. As the concentration of KOH starts increasing from 1 to \(10\,\mathrm{M}\), ethylene begins to evolve at a low potential of \(-0.16\,\mathrm{V}\) vs. RHE on to the \(\mathrm{Cu}\)-GDE [134]. Similarly, Jouny et al. used an \(\mathrm{OD-Cu}\) catalyst in a flow cell to achieve \(C_{2+}\) production with \(91\%\) FE and a current density of \(630\,\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) with a low overpotential of \(-0.67\,\mathrm{V}\) [345]. The excellent FE of \(91\%\) for the \(C_{2+}\) hydrocarbon during \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) is of high industrial importance, but there is a potential disadvantage in terms of limited long-term catalyst stability in GDE with initial \(C_{2+}\) product efficiency. \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) gas that is partially soluble in water reduces the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) rate. This problem has been solved using a novel GDE configuration in which increasing the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) pressure elevates \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) concentration in aqueous electrolytes [346, 347]. However, this method has limitations due to high pressure, such as the deterioration of the \(\mathrm{Cu}\) electrodes and the formation of carbon deposits; scientific explanations for these issues are still pending. Some organic solvents such as acetonitrile, methanol, N,N-dimethylformamide, and ILs have demonstrated \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) solubility that is several orders of magnitude greater than that of aqueous electrolytes. ILs, in particular, exhibit dual behavior in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\). In addition to being an excellent solvent, they can act as a cocatalyst due to their ability to form complexes with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), lowering the activation energy for initiating \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) [348]. For example, in the presence of 1-ethyl-3-methylimidazolium tetrafluoroborate \(\left(\mathrm{Et}_{3}\mathrm{mim}\right)\mathrm{BF}_{4}\)-LiI-water solution as an electrolyte, the reduction of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to acetic acid with FE of \(80.3\%\) occurs on \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{I})\) and a carbon-doped boron nitride composite [349]. The effect of the \(C_{2+}\) product on FE in the presence of different electrolytes is presented in Table 8.
In the presence of a particular electrolyte, the selectivity of \(\mathrm{Cu}\)-based catalysis determines the formation of desirable
products ( \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) and/or \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) ) [353]. The phenomena governing such formation reveal different ion behaviors in different solutions. Alkali metal cations such as \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) , \(\mathrm{Na^{+}}\) , \(\mathsf{K}^{+}\) , and \(C s^{+}\) in bicarbonate solutions, for example, are used to tune the \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) ratio [354]. Because of the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) buffering action, bicarbonate electrolytes operate at near neutral \(\mathrm{pH}\) . Working with varying \(\mathrm{pH}\) in CO, concentrated electrolytes is difficult. Therefore, the effects of bulk \(\mathrm{pH}\) on CO reduction at different \(\mathrm{pH}\) values have been emphasized in numerous studies. At \(\mathrm{pH}\ 6{-}12\) , for example, the reduction of CO motivates \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) formation via a coupled proton–electron transfer mechanism. Unlike \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) , ethylene formation is \(\mathrm{pH}\) independent and is a major hydrocarbon product in alkaline solutions, as opposed to acidic and neutral solutions where \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) formation is preferred [355]. However, the neutral \(\mathrm{pH}\) electrolyte is preferred for efficient \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) . It has also been observed that small changes in proton concentration cause a dramatic change in electrolyte \(\mathrm{pH}\) near the electrode, eventually affecting product selectivity. Gupta et al. [147] demonstrated that the local \(\mathrm{pH}\) of the electrolyte is affected by the thickness of the diffusion layer, current density, and electrolyte buffer capacity. In addition, during \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) , such local \(\mathrm{pH}\) can be increased up to six times higher than the bulk \(\mathrm{pH}\) . Furthermore, Smith et al.
demonstrated that the lengths and densities of the obtained Cu NWs, diffusion of \(\mathrm{HCO}_{3}{}^{-}\) into the Cu NWs, and generation of \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) out of the Cu NWs are all tunable, affecting the local \(\mathrm{pH}\) and overall product selectivity (Fig. 21(g)) [120].
The bicarbonate cations, as previously stated, suppress an increase in electrolyte \(\mathrm{pH}\) near the electrode surface during the catalytic consumption of interfacial protons. The \(\mathrm{pH}\) of the solution, however, varies at the interface. \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) is associated with the release of the \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) ion or consumption of proton. Furthermore, HER kinetics tends to induce an \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) gradient, which raises the \(\mathrm{pH}\) in the electrode vicinity [356]. Varela et al. [357] investigated electrolytes with low buffering strength to demonstrate this phenomenon and discovered a shift in selectivity toward the formation of \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) . The low buffering capacity of electrolytes affects the proton-dependent reaction, involving \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) and HER, whereas \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) production remains unaffected. This phenomenon is attributed to dilute bicarbonate electrolytes' poor buffering capacity [357]. Later, it was discovered that electrolytes with a high pKa value make the \(\mathrm{pH}\) in the electrode vicinity alkaline, and this \(\mathrm{pH}\) difference affects not only the reaction occurring at the electrode interface but also the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) diffusion [358]. The proton-dependent mechanism according to Norskov and colleagues [167, 359] and proton
independent path according to Koper and colleagues [343] have been illustrated in Fig. 21(h). Conversely, Gupta et al. [147] explained the effect of current density on the \(\mathrm{pH}\) of the electrolyte solution. The study proposed a model for displaying high current densities, which results in increased proton consumption and a significant change in local \(\mathrm{pH}\).
At a given electrode potential, the current density on rough surfaces is typically higher than that on smooth surfaces. Rough catalytic surfaces with a thick diffusion layer tend to raise local interfacial \(\mathrm{pH}\), which increases \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) production while suppressing \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) formation [218]. Similarly, Hori et al. investigated \(\mathrm{pH}\)-dependent \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) and its effect on achieving selectivity between \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) [361]. The reaction rates of each product are compared in buffer solutions with bulk \(\mathrm{pH}\) ranging from 6 to 12 [355]. A straight line is obtained when the measured current density of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) or \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{OH}\) is plotted on a single Tafel plot on the standard hydrogen electrode (SHE) scales (Fig. 22(a)). Similarly, Hori et al. investigated \(\mathrm{pH}\) dependence using the Tafel equation, which displays a proportional relation between \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) current density and proton activity. As a result, by plotting the logarithm of the \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) partial current density against \(\mathrm{pH}\) (Fig. 22(b)), a linear relationship between the two is obtained. It is important to note that the transfer coefficients of \(\alpha=0.35\) and 1.33 are taken out for \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\), separately [361]. Hence, the synthesis of \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) occurs via distinct pathways, with rate-determining steps and reaction mechanisms distinct from the initial COR.
Recent research has also compared \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) and CORR on planar polycrystalline \(\mathrm{Cu}\) in an electrolyte with a \(\mathrm{pH}\) range of 6.8–13, providing better understanding of the role of \(\mathrm{pH}\) in reaction mechanisms [362]. To demonstrate the overall effect of \(\mathrm{pH}\), partial current densities for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) and CORR products
are plotted on both the RHE and SHE scales (Figs. 22(c) and 22(d)). The overpotentials of \(\mathrm{C}_{2+}\) products demonstrate that the \(\mathrm{pH}\) is independent of SHE scales. However, it undergoes a much greater shift on the RHE scale from \(\mathrm{pH}\,6.8\) to \(\mathrm{pH}\,13\). Similarly, the overpotentials of \(\mathrm{C}_{1}\) products are dependent on \(\mathrm{pH}\) on SHE scales and exhibit a smaller shift of \(0.15\;\mathrm{V}\) on RHE when compared to \(\mathrm{C}_{2+}\) products. Given the importance of local \(\mathrm{pH}\) in regulating the selectivity during \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) over the \(\mathrm{Cu}\) surface toward \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) formation, there is a critical need to understand the relationship between \(\mathrm{pH}\), catalyst structure, and morphology.
Because \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) is structurally sensitive, as discussed in the previous section, the electrode is constantly exposed to electrolytes for adjusting catalyst operation at an applied potential. Understanding the true nature of catalytically active sites requires extensive spectroscopic and microscopy analyses before, after, and most importantly, during reaction conditions [141, 363]. During \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) in \(0.1\mathrm{~M~KHCO}_{3}\), for example, a polycrystalline \(\mathrm{Cu}\) electrode surface was investigated using ECSTM, and the results revealed significant surface reconstruction from a polycrystalline surface to a well-known ethylene-selective \(\mathrm{Cu}\) (100) surface orientation within \(3\,\mathrm{h}\) at \(-0.9\;\mathrm{V}\) against SHE [364]. These morphological variations are more spontaneous in \(0.1\,\mathrm{~M~}\) KOH, exhibiting transition with \(\mathrm{Cu}\) (111) as the intermediate orientation (Figs. 23(a)–23(c)) [365]. However, very few studies have been conducted to better understand the possible variation in catalyst selectivity over time.
Recent advances in operando characterization techniques have aided in the investigation of dynamic changes on the catalyst surface during operation. For instance, an operando EC-AFM study has revealed the structural disorderness of carbon-supported \(\mathrm{Cu}\) nanocubes and particle size shrinkage in \(0.1\mathrm{~M~KHCO}_{3}\) at different time intervals, influencing \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\)
activity and selectivity [141]. Similarly, even at open-circuit potential (OCV), a plasma-oxidized Cu foil catalyst displays high selectivity toward \(C_{2+}\) products and transformation to CuI particles in the electrolyte containing an \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\)ion. Furthermore, particle morphology can be varied by hosting different cations (Figs. 23(d)–23(f)) [206]. The electrolyte prerestructured catalyst surfaces, for example, exhibit different modifications during \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\). In the KI-containing electrolyte, the CuI crystals are transformed to a highly porous surface. Conversely, in the CsI-containing electrolyte, certain smaller CuI particles maintain their morphology throughout the reaction, facilitating \(\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{C}\) bonds by stabilizing \(\mathrm{{Cu^{+}}}\)species [237]. Furthermore, larger cations \(\mathrm{Cs^{+}}\)vs. \(\mathbf{K}^{+}\)have been shown to aid in the stabilization of CuI species more effectively than the smaller ones.
An electrolyte-driven nanostructuring strategy was used to create effective \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) catalysts, and the dynamic evolution of catalyst structures in an electrolyte at the applied voltage was studied. Cycling electropolished Cu foil in a KCl solution yielded controllable Cu nanocubes with enhanced selectivity and \(73\%\) FE toward \(C_{2}{\mathrm{-}}C_{3}\) hydrocarbons and alcohols [132]. In this regard, varying KCl concentrations can be used to tune the size of Cu nanocubes. In contrast to its similar design using the metal ion battery cycling method [200] or in operando KCl-containing \(\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}\) solution [158], this synthesis method has proven effective in designing morphologically stable
and highly selective Cu (100) facets as a \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) catalyst. Furthermore, \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) nanowires are electrochemically grown on polycrystalline Cu foil via oxidation in concentrated KOH by anodization. Under reaction conditions, \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) nanowires can be further reduced to a Cu catalyst with hybrid oxidation states [228]. These examples show how electrolyte-driven nanostructures can be used to develop novel catalysts.
Similarly, Kley et al. [366] pioneered the use of operando EC-AFM to monitor the dynamic morphological and chemical changes of Cu cubes during \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\). The study reveals dramatic changes in Cu cube morphology supported on C during \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\), including roughening and loss of (100) facets, loss of Cu atoms from edge and corner sites, and a decrease in \(\mathrm{CuO}_{2}\) species. The investigation also reveals that \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) selectivity decreases with decreasing cubic crystal size, which can be attributed to the aforementioned changes in Cu nanocube morphology during \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\). The researchers also used EC-AFM to track the structural evolution of an as-prepared Cu (100) surface from the micrometer to the atomic scale at various cathodic potentials (Figs. 23(g)–23(i)). It can be seen that during \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) in a \(0.1\;\mathrm{M}\;\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}\) electrolyte, potential-dependent transformations of Cu (100) surfaces from grainy to mound-pit surfaces or rectangular terraces occur (Fig. 23(j)). Furthermore, in situ atomic-scale imaging reveals the complex interrelation among the morphology, structure, defect density, applied potential, and electrolyte [366].
## 5 Conclusions and outlook
5 Conclusions and outlookThe use of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) in conjunction with a renewable energy source to produce high energy density \(C_{2+}\) hydrocarbons is a promising strategy for achieving a sustainable society. In this paper, we discussed state-of-the-art \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) on Cu-based catalysts, as well as strategies for tuning \(C_{2+}\) selectivity, such as surface faceting, nanostructure design, metallic alloys, and electrolyte effect. When nanoscale \(\mathrm{Cu}\) is synthesized, its intrinsic catalytic activity remains unaffected and is independent of the method being used. Other nanostructure features, such as surface facet and large surface area, play an important role to tuning the selectivity of \(\mathrm{Cu}\) catalysts. To gain insight into the correlation among the structure, chemical state, and reactivity of a size and shape-controlled \(\mathrm{Cu}\) catalyst during \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\), in situ and operando spectroscopic and microscopic methods have been proposed. The roughening of the nano-Cu surface, disappearance of the (100) facets, formation of pores, loss of \(\mathrm{Cu}\) facet, and reduction of \(\mathrm{CuO}_{x}\) species under reaction conditions result in the decreased selectivity for multicarbon products (e.g., \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) and ethanol) vs. \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\). Furthermore, the loss of nanostructure morphology during \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) has been found to affect long-term catalyst activity. The nature of electrolytes also plays an important role in the stability and selectivity of the catalyst, and a small change in electrolyte \(\mathrm{pH}\) is likely to change the mechanism or rate of \(C_{2+}\) product formation. Furthermore, the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) in the presence of a \(\mathrm{Cu}\) catalyst shows great promise in the selective formation of \(C_{2+}\) products with high FE. Regardless, much more work is still needed to ensure long-term catalytic stability with improved performance toward \(C_{2+}\) products, as well as cost-effectiveness and large-scale implementation to make \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) commercially viable. To summarize, there are numerous challenges and opportunities to better understand the fundamental mechanisms of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\). With continued research and development, the electrochemical reduction of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) can have a profound impact on the sustainability of our global energy storage economy.
Cu-derived materials have high potential for use in next-generation \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion systems. In the light of their roles discussed in this study, recent developments and advancements in Cu-based catalysts for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) have made their contribution clear in obtaining \(C_{2+}\) products. However, numerous breakthroughs in the design, synthesis, characterization, and deployment of Cu-based materials for critical applications have been proposed. Despite the advances made in the last two decades, this field is still in its early stages. However, unresolved issues must be addressed to advance \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion systems.
Furthermore, low-dimensional \(\mathrm{Cu}\) and Cu-derived materials offer promising routes for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\). Tailoring the electronic properties of SACs, MOFs, Cu interfaces, and polymer-incorporated \(\mathrm{Cu}\) catalyst through surface engineering aids in the development of highly selective and efficient catalysts for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion into valuable hydrocarbons. Furthermore, the combined experimental and theoretical studies aided in the evolution of novel hybrid and low-dimensional heterostructures via doping and alloying to improve thermodynamics kinetics during \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\). Although significant efforts have been made to achieve ideal catalysts for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction, much research is still required to understand the catalyst's working mechanism, morphological variations, and degradation mechanism during electrocatalysis. Despite numerous efforts to improve \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) activity and selectivity, very little research has been conducted on the nature and number of active sites of the catalysts. In terms of active sites, novel in situ characterization techniques with high precision are critical for gaining insight into catalyst active sites. However, using characterization techniques during complex \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) is difficult. Ex situ analysis results in inconceivable variations in oxidation state, structure, and morphology on exposure to air, but it is difficult to make conclusive statements regarding active sites. Aside from identifying active sites, current research involves increasing the number of active sites by increasing electrode surface, resulting in increased electrode–electrolyte interactions and thus increasing current density.
