In situ operando characterization techniques are in high demand for developing a rational \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) catalyst and electrolyte designs. In situ XAFS analysis, for instance, has confirmed a direct correlation between ethylene formation and the presence of \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\)species [224]. In addition to XAFS analysis, quasi-in situ XPS studies tend to further correlate the selectivity of \(C_{2+}\) products with \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\)contents that are stabilized over \(\mathrm{Cu}\) surface/nanocubes [132, 141]. Similarly, the presence of halide, subsurface oxygen species, and electrolyte cations contributes to the selectivity of \(C_{2+}\) products and enhances the catalyst working operation [350]. Conversely, the role of subsurface oxygen species in Cu-based electrocatalysts during \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) remains unclear. These constraints have prompted researchers to develop highly efficient and stable catalysts [204]. Despite current progress, more advances in operando characterization techniques suitable for liquid environments under \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) conditions are prospective. Furthermore, there is an urgent need to improve the signal intensity/strength of in situ scattering and spectroscopic techniques such as XAS [224], XPS [141], FTIR [98], Raman [229, 230], DEMS [360], EC-AFM [141], EC-STM [365], and liquid-TEM [367]. These advancements will help with the precise identification of active sites, catalyst degradation mechanics, and key reaction intermediates, as well as in defining distinct reaction pathways for different electrode and electrolyte combinations.
Improving the nature of the Cu electrode material results in increased efficiency with long-term operation. In terms of the recent advancement, such milestones have already been achieved on a lab-scale in low-dimensional Cu and their hybrids (SACs, MOFs, Cu interface, and polymer-incorporated Cu). For large-scale deployment, factors such as catalyst reproducibility, coherent understanding of the reaction mechanism, and long-term stability in an industrial environment should be thoroughly considered. Overall, Cu-based electrocatalysts have demonstrated their capabilities by providing promising prospects in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) to \(C_{2+}\) hydrocarbon conversion. However, economically effective strategies for large-scale \(C_{2+}\) product synthesis have been rarely reported [368], implying that low-cost and innovative strategies will be required in the future development of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) systems [369].
